WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.199
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:21.289
This is Tim Ferriss for a rare in-person podcast.

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:26.230
I know that's become the norm on YouTube and
elsewhere, but this is a rare occasion and

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.460
I am thrilled to have Andrew Huberman here
with me.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.950
So great to have you here in person, Andrew.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:35.620
So Andrew Huberman, who is this Andrew Huberman?

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.250
Dr. Huberman, PhD, on Twitter @HubermanLab,
is a neuroscientist and tenured professor

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.010
in the Department of Neurobiology at Stanford
University's School of Medicine.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.949
He has made numerous important contributions
to the fields of brain development, brain

00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:49.680
function, and neuroplasticity.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.430
Work from the Huberman Laboratory at Stanford
Medicine has been consistently published in

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.859
top journals including nature, science and
cell.

00:00:56.859 --> 00:01:00.480
For those who don't know, that's like having
a sweep at the Oscars.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.019
But back to the bio, Andrew's the host of
the podcast Huberman Lab, which is often ranked

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:17.840
as one of the top five podcasts in the world
by both Apple and Spotify.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.259
The show aims to help viewers and listeners
improve their health with science and science-based

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:23.259
tools.

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:26.590
New episodes air every Monday on YouTube and
all podcast platforms.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:33.130
You can find all things Andrew at hubermanlab.com,
on YouTube that is Huberman Lab, Instagram,

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.409
HubermanLab, and also on Twitter as mentioned
@HubermanLab.

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:37.119
Andrew, nice to see you.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:39.799
Andrew Huberman: Great to see you.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:40.939
So happy to be here.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:45.299
I want to say I grew up listening to your
podcast, although I think I was in my 30s

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:46.299
when I started listening.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:51.619
And so for me, I'm really tickled to be here
because so much of how I ran my laboratory

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:56.150
when I first became a professor was based
on principles from The 4-Hour Workweek.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.250
Now mind you, my work weeks were like 100-hour
work weeks.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:03.470
I actually lived in my laboratory with my
dog — my students and postdocs can attest

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.420
to that — but I incorporated a ton of the
principles.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.750
I was following The 4-Hour Body slow carb
diet.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.099
I was training.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:13.860
I had my cheat days and on and on and on.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:19.349
So for me, this is kind of being transported
forward and back in time.

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:20.370
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.370
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.209
Well, I can't say enough positive things about
you and your podcast and what you've done,

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:28.050
and as you know, this is not just because
I'm sitting here in front of you because I

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:29.120
text you all the time.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:36.270
In fact, I will say this, I have a notebook
that dates back over a decade where at the

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.390
time I was pretty lonely.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.870
It was just me and my dog.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:42.500
Eventually, a great girlfriend at the time
came along.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.720
But I was running my lab and there's a lot
of social buffers between professors and students,

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.720
understandably and necessarily.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.379
And so I was pretty lonely, and I thought,
who are my friends going to be?

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:57.370
I was in a new town, I didn't know many people,
and I have this list, and I read the list

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:02.569
the other day, I'll send you a photo, and
the list was of about five or six people that

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:07.640
I really admired and whose principles and
work I was trying to incorporate into basically

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.420
every aspect of my life.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.019
At the top of the list is a guy named Tim
Armstrong, lead singer for Rancid, who I've

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:15.380
recently become friends with.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.290
And so totally, that's another amazing story.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:23.849
Joe Strummer, who unfortunately I never met,
the great Joe Strummer, which explains my

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:24.849
attire.

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:28.930
He always wore a buttoned-down black shirt
in his adulthood, so I decided to do that.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:33.390
At some point and much more related to your
name, because The 4-Hour Workweek and your

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:39.159
podcast which eventually came along, Rick
Rubin, who I've had the great blessing of

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:43.700
having on my podcast and learning from, and
then Oliver Sacks, who unfortunately passed

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.510
away before I ever had the chance to meet
him.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:54.000
But anyway, I had to tell you that you were
already my friend before you knew who I was.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.080
And I did that because I would look at that
list and think, okay, who do I want to try

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.450
and embody in terms of ways about going through
life and trying to do things right in my professional

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:05.450
and personal life?

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.000
So that was my shortlist, and I'm very proud
of that shortlist.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.000
I still have it.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.010
I sent Tim the list the other day and he was
like, "No way, man.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.010
This is pretty wild."

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.109
And I'm like, "You think it's wild for you,
just imagine how wild this is for me."

00:04:16.109 --> 00:04:17.690
So anyway, to set the context.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:23.190
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, well, I appreciate that,
man, and I've been incredibly impressed, not

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:27.740
just with your research and academic bonafides
and what you've accomplished there, which

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:28.930
is a lot in and of itself.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:35.660
And of course we've spoken before, but the
incredible focus and force with which you

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:42.770
have just blown the barn doors off with the
podcast, which is really a service to people.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:48.280
So I am happy to have you here, thrilled to
be spending time in person after COVID and

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.120
recording remotely.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:56.009
So we have a ton to dive into, and hopefully
I will not be the hero with clay feet.

00:04:56.009 --> 00:05:02.050
As they say, it's all downhill from here as
far as what I can do in this conversation.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:08.640
But I thought we could begin with perhaps
revisiting in some respects, our last conversation,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.880
not to rehash it, but to simply ask the question.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.919
Since we last spoke, which was a while ago,
I guess it was about maybe two years ago,

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:22.550
what have you changed your mind on and what
have you doubled down on if you have answers

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:23.550
to both of those?

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.660
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I'll start with what
I've doubled down on.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:33.130
I've doubled down on the idea, which perhaps
I stated last time we spoke, and perhaps not.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:41.319
But I certainly believe that our state of
mind and body at any point in time is strongly

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:47.270
dictated by our state of mind and body in
the hours and days prior to that.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:50.690
And on the one hand, people are going to hear
that and say, "Well duh, if you're sleep-deprived,

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.060
you're going to feel like garbage.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.270
And if you're well-rested, you'll feel great."

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:57.240
That's kind of the top contour of it.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.569
But when one looks at the neuroscience, for
instance, of sleep, you start to realize that

00:06:02.569 --> 00:06:06.400
the amount of rapid eye movement sleep that
you're going to get in any 90-minute bout

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.220
of sleep, because your sleep is broken up
into these 90-minute segments, more or less,

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:17.259
is strongly dictated by the ratio of slow
wave sleep, AKA deep sleep and rapid eye movement

00:06:17.259 --> 00:06:19.819
sleep that you had in the previous 90-minute
bout.

00:06:19.819 --> 00:06:25.009
And then when you start to look at the research
in terms of waking states, you start to find

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:30.259
that your ability to be focused, say, for
a bout of work in the morning or the afternoon,

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:35.139
or a creative brainstorm session, or I don't
know, to maybe drill into some personal issue

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:39.849
that you're dealing with during therapy or
just on a walk or while journaling is not

00:06:39.849 --> 00:06:41.790
a square wave function.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.160
None of us should sit down and expect ourselves
to just drop into that state.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:50.860
Much of our ability to move into that state
effectively, whatever effective means, whatever

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:57.349
the target or goal of that bout as I'm calling
it, is going to be dictated by what happened

00:06:57.349 --> 00:07:00.770
in the previous moments and hours.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:06.319
And so when I zoom out from that, what I've
doubled down on in this is this idea that

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:12.080
there are just a core set of foundational
things that we have to re-up every 24 hours.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:19.069
I think thanks to the incredibly hard work
of Dr. Matt Walker at Berkeley, The Sleep

00:07:19.069 --> 00:07:22.949
Diplomat on Twitter, it's such a great name
because it's so appropriate.

00:07:22.949 --> 00:07:26.789
A decade ago or so, it was like, "I'll sleep
when I'm dead."

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:28.599
That was the dominant mentality out there.

00:07:28.599 --> 00:07:32.350
And yeah, sleep's great, but getting stuff
done is more important.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:35.879
Matt has really impressed on everybody that
our mental health, our physical health, and

00:07:35.879 --> 00:07:41.949
our ability to perform is so strongly dependent
on our ability to get quality sleep — maybe

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:45.909
not every night of our life; we have to be
realistic — but that sleep is vital.

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:48.530
So a hat tip is insufficient.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:53.840
So sleep is critical, but sleep is just one
of about, I would say five things that really

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.030
set the buoyancy or the foundation upon which
our nervous system is able to accomplish these

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.030
transitions that I'm talking about at all.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:10.379
And those five things are sleep — in the
absence of quality sleep over two or three

00:08:10.379 --> 00:08:12.629
days, you're just going to fall to pieces.

00:08:12.629 --> 00:08:17.139
In the presence of quality-sufficient sleep
over two or three days, you're going to function

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:19.759
at an amazing level.

00:08:19.759 --> 00:08:21.900
There's a gain of function and a loss of function
there.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.120
It's not just if you sleep poorly, you function
less well.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.780
You sleep better, you function much better.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:30.300
So sleep, I would say, is at the top of the
list.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:36.589
Nutrients, and there you can think macronutrients
and so your carnivores are only eating meat

00:08:36.589 --> 00:08:41.669
and your vegans are only eating plants and
your omnivores, which is I think probably

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:43.770
90 percent of the world, is eating a combination
of those.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:50.250
But quality nutrients, I think that when I
look at all of the nutrition, literature,

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.680
and arguments out there, it seems that everyone
can agree on one thing, which is that probably

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.460
80 percent or more of our nutrition should
come from unprocessed or minimally processed

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:00.460
sources.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.100
Minimally processed would require some cooking,
but it survive on the shelf as opposed to

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.370
packaged foods or highly palatable foods.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.380
So you've got sleep nutrients.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:12.560
But then we should also put in micronutrients,
and this is where maybe we'll get into a discussion

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:13.560
about supplementation.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:18.400
I think that there's supplementation or supplements
is a bit of a misnomer because it implies

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.990
vitamin supplements and people say, "Well,
can't you get all that from food or that whey

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:24.490
protein, isn't that just food?

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:25.890
Wouldn't you be better off with a chicken
breast?"

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:29.360
Okay, well then when you talk about convenience
and the absorption, okay, but then there's

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:35.790
this huge category of things ranging from
the kind of esoterically named things like

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.710
Ashwagandha and Shilajit and tongkat ali and
Fadogia agrestis —

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:39.710
Tim Ferriss: I'm in!

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:40.710
I'll buy all of them!

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:41.710
Andrew Huberman: Exactly.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:42.710
All the herbal stuff, right?

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:43.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:44.710
Andrew Huberman: You're not going to get that
from food.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:45.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:46.710
Andrew Huberman: So should we call them supplements
at all?

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.149
And so let's just say the third thing is,
or the second thing is nutrients, and that

00:09:49.149 --> 00:09:53.050
includes macronutrients, and that includes
micronutrients as well.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.050
So those two things.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:56.540
Then the third would be movement.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.310
And this has also been an enormous transition
in the last, I think just five years, which

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:05.210
is not just for people interested in body
building or power lifting or for competitive

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:12.329
athletes, but now it seems everybody, including
the elderly, understand that you need a combination

00:10:12.329 --> 00:10:17.060
of cardiovascular exercise and you need resistance
training, whether or not it's with body weight

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:20.360
or weights or machines, etc, that you need
both.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:24.839
Not a week goes by without seeing an article
in one of the major publications out there,

00:10:24.839 --> 00:10:28.050
standard media, let's call it traditional
media, we'll be nice to them, traditional

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:32.570
media that highlights some study showing that
resistance training in elderly people can

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:38.510
offset Alzheimer's or that as our friend Peter
Attia has pointed out so many times that many

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:44.430
of the end-of-life-creating injuries are due
to older people stepping down, the eccentric

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:45.430
movements.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:47.730
So you need movement, that's the third category.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:53.740
Fourth, I will argue, and I like to think
that maybe I've helped this movement, if you

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.529
want to call it that, is light.

00:10:55.529 --> 00:11:00.080
In particular, sunlight in the early part
and throughout the middle of the day, and

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:03.620
trying to minimize the amount of artificial
light that you're exposed to in the evening

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:08.639
and late night hours most of the time, because
you have to live life, just fundamental.

00:11:08.639 --> 00:11:14.230
And then I think the last category that's
important is social connection, AKA relationships,

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:17.649
let's just call it relationships, because
that can include relationship to self.

00:11:17.649 --> 00:11:21.589
So those things set up the core foundation.

00:11:21.589 --> 00:11:25.880
And I think one way to think about them is
just as a list.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:29.360
Another is to think about them in terms of
a schedule basis.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.440
And that's how I've really doubled down is
I realize that every 24 hours I need to invest

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:36.959
something into each one of those things.

00:11:36.959 --> 00:11:40.700
So I think that 10 years ago, or five years
ago, or even two years ago, I used to think,

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:42.690
okay, what's the workout split?

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:46.410
Or how am I going to eat for the next couple
of months?

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:47.980
What am I trying to optimize for?

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:48.980
Is it muscle?

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:49.980
Is it fat loss?

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:50.980
Is it just maintaining?

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:51.980
Is it energy?

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:52.980
Is it focus?

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:53.980
That's all fine and good.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:12:01.779
But sleep, nutrients, exercise, light, relationships,
those really establish the foundation of what

00:12:01.779 --> 00:12:08.420
I consider to be all of the elements that
create our ability to move as seamlessly as

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:13.010
possible between the states that we happen
to be in and the states we desire to be in.

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:17.230
And when I zoom out and I think about what
are the major struggles that I, and it seems

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:20.070
everyone deals with, it's like how to get
more focused.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:23.320
So we can talk about what do you take, what's
the supplement?

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.420
But you have to say, "Well, how are you sleeping?

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:26.420
Have you done any exercise?"

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:31.700
You really always find yourself, or I find
myself taking 10 steps back and then moving

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:35.310
through the sequence of five things before
you can even begin to talk about whether or

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:40.250
not taking three or 600 milligrams of Alpha
GPC and how often to do that and does it work,

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:41.440
and yes, it works, etc.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.880
But those things really set the foundation.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:47.339
And so I like to think of those five things
every single day.

00:12:47.339 --> 00:12:52.460
You have to re-up on sleep every 24 hours
or try to, you have to re-up on movement every

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:53.460
24 hours.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.870
You can go a day or so immobile, but you better
move the next day.

00:12:57.870 --> 00:12:59.700
And ideally, you're moving seven days a week.

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:03.010
Doesn't necessarily mean trained to failure
and running a marathon seven days a week.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:06.579
You can Goggins your life or you can not Goggins
your life.

00:13:06.579 --> 00:13:10.060
For those of you who don't know, I'm referring
to David Goggins there, by the way, who seems

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:11.080
to never stop moving.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:14.350
Although I just learned meditates two hours
every night, every night.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:18.170
And I'm inclined to believe when he says that,
that he indeed does that.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:21.930
You need nutrients, even if they come from
stored sources, even if you're going to fast.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:25.269
You're going to fast for a day or two, okay
fine, I've done that.

00:13:25.269 --> 00:13:27.380
I know you've done that.

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.430
I would put hydration under nutrients too.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:33.199
So you can drive nutrients from stored fat,
protein, etc, glycogen.

00:13:33.199 --> 00:13:37.760
The relationship it's light is you're going
to need that every 24 hours.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:42.430
You're going to need sunlight, even if through
cloud cover, and you're going to want to avoid

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:46.370
bright artificial lights at night, not every
night, but most nights of your life.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.399
And then that relationship's one is the one
that maybe we can go into in a little bit

00:13:49.399 --> 00:13:55.170
more depth at some point, but it requires
focus, it requires attention, every 24 hours.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:59.740
Now, that doesn't necessarily mean you have
to see friends, talk to friends, text friends

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:00.740
every 24 hours.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:04.269
Some people are far more introverted than
others, but then you're working on your relationship

00:14:04.269 --> 00:14:08.690
to yourself in that solo time, and hopefully
when you're spending time with others as well,

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:10.660
that has some internal repercussions.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:15.990
So if I've doubled down on anything, it's
the understanding that there is no so-called

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.200
optimization.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:22.829
There is no real interest, at least from me,
in trying to layer in other things unless

00:14:22.829 --> 00:14:26.389
I'm paying attention to each and every one
of those things every 24 hours.

00:14:26.389 --> 00:14:30.930
You have to re-up on each and every one of
those five things every 24 hours.

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:35.990
And if you don't, you can get by for a day
or two, but pretty soon you're going to hit

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:40.900
that wall where you won't be able to do any
of the things that most people are actually

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:42.040
seeking to do.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:46.290
And the last thing I'll say about that is
I think people hear a list of those five things

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:50.440
and they think, "Gosh, okay, well that must
be nice for you, Andrew and Tim.

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:56.850
You wake up, you look at sunlight, you guys
don't have kids, you don't have to worry about

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:57.850
kids running around.

00:14:57.850 --> 00:14:58.850
You can exercise."

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:04.690
There are ways of layering in the protocols
that re-up as I'm referring to, these five

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:12.910
things every 24 hours that also include other
people in your life, kids, pets, etc.

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:14.960
Exercise certainly can include that as well.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:20.490
But I would argue that there is no showing
up properly for yourself and for the other

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.440
people in your life unless these things are
being handled.

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.220
And it's not about becoming soft and cushy,
it's actually about becoming quite resilient

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.220
and effective.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:36.260
And I think this for me it seems so simple,
but as our friend Paul Conti said to me recently,

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:40.930
he said, "After all the analysis and pouring
through things and the complicated notions

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:45.440
of the subconscious…" he's a psychiatrist,
after all, "...in the end, really great mental

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:50.110
health is about simple practices, like first
principles of self-care."

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:54.880
So to which I raised my hand and said, "Well,
what is a first principle of self-care?

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:55.990
I'm a biologist, after all."

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:56.990
And he said, "Aha.

00:15:56.990 --> 00:15:59.279
It's basically the things that we were just
talking about."

00:15:59.279 --> 00:16:00.660
There's those five things.

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:07.110
And so I'm doubling down, I'm tripling down
on those as essential to the point where nothing

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:11.420
else really happens for very long unless those
five things are tended to.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:12.500
Tim Ferriss: Question.

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:19.120
Of those five, let's just say, if you had
to pick one that you were — neglecting may

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.510
be a strong word, but underweighting, that
you are now weighting differently, what would

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:27.060
it be and what have you done?

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:29.560
What have you added or changed or subtracted?

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:32.630
Andrew Huberman: I'll have to pick two.

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:33.630
Let's do —
Tim Ferriss: Both, then.

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:34.630
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:41.570
The two are movement, really changed the way
that I train and exercise to some extent.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:46.959
And actually my whole philosophy on what's
possible in terms of training and how to incorporate

00:16:46.959 --> 00:16:53.139
it into a week in a way that really works
to build strength and endurance and feel really

00:16:53.139 --> 00:16:57.640
good in one's body all the time.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:04.500
And then the other one is relationships, which
probably reflects place in life where I'm

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:05.500
47 now.

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:09.740
I've chosen to delay having a family, but
that's a primary focus.

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:20.299
But also having done a lot of personal work
toward my mid-40s, I thought I was "there."

00:17:20.299 --> 00:17:23.079
And then realizing that —
Tim Ferriss: It's a trap door.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:24.079
Andrew Huberman: It's a trap door.

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:31.140
And then realizing that, I guess here again,
I'll use a language that Paul uses, which

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:34.510
is that there were some unresolved core conflicts.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:40.890
And this idea of core conflicts is really,
I think, the most appropriate way to put the

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:46.480
important psychological stuff that people
need to work through.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:52.150
Everyone has them, many people have trauma,
not everyone has trauma, but as defined as

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:56.870
an event that fundamentally changes the way
that you nervous system works, such that you

00:17:56.870 --> 00:17:59.620
function less well in some or many domains
of life.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:02.480
Again, I've robbed that definition from Paul
Conti.

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:07.900
I'm far less eloquent than he is in delivering
it, but I think realizing that there's still

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:11.550
some core conflicts that I needed to resolve.

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:16.490
And I'm going whole hog on that, and it's
been interesting to say the least.

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:20.750
Tim Ferriss: So let's start with perhaps the
easier one.

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.020
Movement.

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:23.200
What have you implemented?

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:29.650
What have you embraced or cut back on in the
movement category?

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:36.500
And I'm very interested in this personally
because I have really taken this as one of

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:41.650
a few of those five to focus on myself in
the last, I would say year.

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:45.430
Because I've done a lot of training, let's
just say in the last 10 years, but I've not

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:51.230
done a lot of competition and I miss developing
athleticism.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:58.520
And if I take as an assumption that we have
largely evolved to move through space to actually

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:03.919
move and navigate, ski touring is just one
example.

00:19:03.919 --> 00:19:09.240
So putting on skins, these are actually, they
used to be actual animal skins, now they're

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:14.510
synthetic, but you put on skins on the bottom
of skis and you effectively NordicTrack your

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:19.520
way up the mountain with switchbacks, and
then you take them off, you do a transition,

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:21.530
you ski down, you rinse and repeat.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:27.380
But the experience of being, if you choose
your environments in a location where you

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:34.120
get lots of sunlight in the morning and early
afternoon, symmetrical exercise, movement

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:39.789
where you're not too heavily weighting one
side or the other, there are benefits to asymmetrical

00:19:39.789 --> 00:19:40.960
types of exercise.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:46.990
But I have found this to just be absolutely,
I don't think it's an overstatement to say

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:51.320
revolutionary for my physical and mental well-being.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:56.799
And you also get, in this particular case
for me, a degree of hip extension that I really

00:19:56.799 --> 00:20:02.120
just do not get in my day-to-day existence
otherwise.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:07.480
So I'm putting that out there just as an example
and an explanation for why I want to dig a

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:08.950
little bit deeper on the movement side.

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.130
So what have you ended up implementing?

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:14.740
Andrew Huberman: Well, first of all, let me
just say that your statement about movement

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:21.490
being so fundamental to who we are as a species,
the Nobel Prize-winning — physiologist is

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:26.570
really what he was — Sherrington said that,
"The final common path of the entire nervous

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:28.409
system is movement."

00:20:28.409 --> 00:20:36.350
Which I sometimes think about because we often
think that our emotions somehow impact the

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:41.520
world, but they really don't, except insofar
as we say things or do things.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:46.850
The other way to put this is the evolutionary
biologist will say, "There is no fossil record

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:49.520
for the brain."

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:53.530
If you look at brains, it's only what people
actually did with the internal architecture

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:54.530
of their brain.

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:57.559
It all boils down to movement or vision, I
would say, because I'm a vision scientist.

00:20:57.559 --> 00:21:02.380
But when you look cubic millimeter by cubic
millimeter through the brain, if you take

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:06.441
the circuits devoted to movement and the circuits
devoted to vision, you've got about 75 percent

00:21:06.441 --> 00:21:08.360
of the human brain.

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:09.950
So that's a lot.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.649
The rest is important too, of course.

00:21:12.649 --> 00:21:13.649
Movement wise.

00:21:13.649 --> 00:21:14.649
Okay, so we did a guest series.

00:21:14.649 --> 00:21:18.529
This was a six-episode guest series with Dr.
Andy Galpin, who's a professor of kinesiology,

00:21:18.529 --> 00:21:23.280
Cal State Fullerton, and his laboratory works
on, does everything from muscle biopsy all

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:26.320
the way up to working with competitive athletes.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:31.740
So they'll do deadlifts or boxing or whatever
it is, or students running on treadmills,

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:36.130
huge range of subjects, and then they'll stab
out some muscle — the little cork of muscle.

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:37.410
And you've had this done right?

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:38.410
This was in The 4-Hour Body.

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:45.309
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I did a muscle biopsy and
videotaped the entire process in the Sports

00:21:45.309 --> 00:21:47.180
Science Institute of South Africa.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:49.800
"Tim tartar" is what I called it when it came
out.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:50.800
So good.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:56.520
Turns out my muscle enzymes are, if it's possible
to be below some type of graph representing

00:21:56.520 --> 00:22:02.440
Homer Simpson, like citrate synthase and these
various elements, it would be very helpful

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:04.010
for endurance which I seem to lack.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:06.159
Andrew Huberman: Bur you're built for explosiveness.

00:22:06.159 --> 00:22:11.039
Tim Ferriss: I'm built for a very short-duration
explosiveness, which is ironic when you consider

00:22:11.039 --> 00:22:16.610
that I'm embracing ski touring because I am
— I'm not like Kílian Jornet or any of

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:19.279
these folks who would be very well-built for
such a thing.

00:22:19.279 --> 00:22:21.789
Andrew Huberman: Well, we make a good team
because I've never had a muscle biopsy.

00:22:21.789 --> 00:22:25.830
But I assure you that when I start running
distance, I can progress very fast.

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:29.090
I'm not particularly strong, I'm not particularly
weak, but I'm just not particularly strong

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:35.610
and very little explosiveness, very little
hops, which is why skateboarding wasn't the

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.710
right sport for me.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:41.490
Despite my deep desire, it just didn't happen.

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:46.250
But in terms of what I learned from Andy,
a couple of key principles fell out of the,

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:50.409
and keep in mind, these are peer-reviewed
studies from his laboratory and many other

00:22:50.409 --> 00:22:52.299
laboratories of which he's an expert.

00:22:52.299 --> 00:22:57.070
And I went deep into this literature with
him for that series.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:23:03.730
Concurrent training, meaning getting better
at distance and getting stronger is absolutely

00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:04.730
possible.

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:05.990
I did not think that was possible.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:10.559
I'm a big fan of the late Charles Poliquin
and others who said, "You want to build muscle,

00:23:10.559 --> 00:23:11.559
build muscle.

00:23:11.559 --> 00:23:12.559
You want to be a runner, be a runner."

00:23:12.559 --> 00:23:15.510
And I think at the extremes, that's true.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.930
But the data really pointed to the fact that
you can train for many things concurrently.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:26.010
I took a step back from everything I learned
from Andy over the last few years in that

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:32.330
series and resculpted my training program
so that on any one given day, I'm training

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:37.320
for something very specific with the understanding
that one can make progress in a lot of different

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:38.400
domains of fitness.

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:42.500
In fact, the way that Andy puts it, I think,
is better than fitness.

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:47.380
He says techniques and methods are many, but
they're only a few set of core adaptations

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:48.380
that your body can make.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:50.110
So you're really just trying to create adaptations.

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:53.910
Whether or not you do it with a kettlebell
or a bar or a dumbbell or a hammer strength

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:55.320
machine doesn't really matter.

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:59.320
You're trying to create certain adaptations
by using certain loads, moving things at different

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:00.320
speeds.

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:03.110
But that is also true of endurance and running.

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:08.180
So what I figured out was that there's an
optimal training schedule for me that allows

00:24:08.180 --> 00:24:10.650
me to target one specific thing each day.

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:13.440
Tim Ferriss: What are some of those specific
things?

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:20.390
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, endurance, strength,
hypertrophy, VO2 max, heat and cold tolerance.

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:22.740
I can talk about why that is.

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:27.800
And also, I should mention each one of those
days, and I can spell this out very, very

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:34.110
simply for you, each one of those days is
also designed to indirectly support one of

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:37.350
the other adaptations I'm trying to accomplish.

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:42.360
So let me explain in short form, and if you
want more detail, I can give you more detail.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:48.420
My training week starts on Sunday because
Sunday sits leftmost on the calendar.

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:55.559
Sunday, I make it a focused effort to move
as much as possible, ideally outdoors.

00:24:55.559 --> 00:24:57.990
I'm thinking endurance.

00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:01.390
I essentially want to be like a mule.

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:03.200
I'm just thinking "Be like a mule."

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.549
I actually have this shirt —
Tim Ferriss: That's going to be in the headline

00:25:05.549 --> 00:25:06.549
of this episode.

00:25:06.549 --> 00:25:07.549
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:25:07.549 --> 00:25:10.520
I actually have this shirt that I sometimes
like to wear on those days.

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:14.029
It's not a black buttoned-down shirt, but
it has a picture of a sloth and it's crossing

00:25:14.029 --> 00:25:18.230
its three sloth fingers like Wolverine, and
that's like what I'm trying to embody.

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:20.500
I'm trying to embody the sloth.

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:24.620
So what I'll do on that day is, because sometimes
it's a social day with other people in my

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:28.399
life, if I'm on my own, I'll throw on an eight
or 10-pound weight vest.

00:25:28.399 --> 00:25:33.149
They have these thinner ones now that aren't
these mirror vests that where you don't look

00:25:33.149 --> 00:25:38.380
like you're in law enforcement or you're trying
to pretend you're in the seal teams, which

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:39.740
I'm not, never was.

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:41.570
But they have these thinner ones that sit
a bit more flush.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:45.130
I forget the brand name now, but I don't have
any relationship to them.

00:25:45.130 --> 00:25:47.890
I'll get it for you, but I really like this
one.

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:54.260
And I'll head out for probably a 75-minute
to a 90-minute slow jog with some hills.

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:55.860
And I'll try and nasal breathe the whole time.

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:58.070
I'll often listen to a podcast or a book.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:25:59.210
Sometimes I'll just let my mind drift.

00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:01.799
That's if I'm on my own.

00:26:01.799 --> 00:26:05.330
If I'm with other people, what I will do is
I'll fill up a backpack with a bunch of heavy

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:08.990
stuff, usually some water in there too, and
drink it as I go.

00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:13.559
And I'll do three or four or five hours of
just hiking and just trying to be outside

00:26:13.559 --> 00:26:15.450
as much as possible.

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:19.220
The specific goal of that day is endurance.

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:20.279
Just keep going.

00:26:20.279 --> 00:26:24.500
And what I notice is because of the other
things I do in the previous days, the 10 or

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:27.990
20 minutes, which come at the start, really
suck.

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:31.520
I either want to go faster, like these little
aches and things.

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:37.050
But what's amazing is somewhere in that 25,
30-minute period, you start to feel really

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:38.050
good.

00:26:38.050 --> 00:26:39.600
You actually start to adapt to it right then.

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:41.460
You kind of go, "Okay, this is about the heart
rate I'll use.

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:43.080
This is about the breathing rate.

00:26:43.080 --> 00:26:46.140
So this is zone —
Tim Ferriss: What is happening at that point

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:48.210
physiologically or neurologically or both?

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:50.169
Andrew Huberman: I'm glad you mentioned neurologically.

00:26:50.169 --> 00:26:53.410
I think physiologically, they're the standard
things that happen during exercise.

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:56.650
You're getting warmer, so joints are more
fluid.

00:26:56.650 --> 00:27:03.299
If your cardiovascular system is able to fuel
the relevant muscles, but you're not over,

00:27:03.299 --> 00:27:05.880
not shuttling too much fuel to specific muscle
groups, etc.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:07.630
Because of course, I could be stressed when
I start that.

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:11.620
I could be relaxed, I could be tired, depending
on how well slept the night before.

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:17.170
But neurologically, what happens is really
important, and we know this from data, what

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:20.500
you're starting to do is you're starting to
incorporate what are called central pattern

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:21.500
generators.

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:25.039
Central pattern generators are what allow
you to engage in a repeated movement without

00:27:25.039 --> 00:27:26.830
voluntary attention to it.

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:32.289
Very different than say, squatting or front
squatting or doing curls or something where

00:27:32.289 --> 00:27:34.299
you're trying to focus on each rep, fundamentally
different.

00:27:34.299 --> 00:27:38.920
Tim Ferriss: So it's like the autopilot button
appears on your steering wheel after 30 minutes..

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:40.240
Andrew Huberman: That's right.

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:43.429
And at that point your mind can really attend
to other things.

00:27:43.429 --> 00:27:50.330
And of course, as your body warms up, you're
also able to achieve much more output.

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:55.450
So you actually are getting better and more
efficient as you progress.

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:56.500
Now that that's weird.

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:57.500
Most exercise doesn't work out.

00:27:57.500 --> 00:27:58.500
Tim Ferriss: It is weird.

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:05.140
But as an intrepid, pseudo-endurance athlete
who's at least really embraced this ski touring,

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:12.230
I've done a lot of it in the last six weeks,
the first 30 to 45 minutes are generally terrible

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:13.620
for everybody.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:20.850
And then you click into a rhythm and you feel
like you've accessed an extra set of batteries.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.370
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:25.690
It's neural, and I think Andy would agree,
it's neural.

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:28.669
You're engaging the proper amount of what
are called upper motor — you have upper

00:28:28.669 --> 00:28:30.690
motor neurons and lower motor neurons.

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:33.330
Lower motor neurons reside in the ventral
spinal cord.

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:35.840
They're the ones that degenerate in ALS.

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:39.769
They're the ones that, fortunately most people
don't degenerate and cause contractions of

00:28:39.769 --> 00:28:40.880
the muscle fibers.

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:43.850
They are directed by upper motor neurons,
which are the ones in your brain that allow

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:46.299
you to generate voluntary movement.

00:28:46.299 --> 00:28:52.409
However, the upper motor neurons and the lower
motor neurons are happy to engage in a central

00:28:52.409 --> 00:28:59.710
pattern generator type circuit if you carry
out something repetitively for long enough.

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:04.770
So as you are able to take your mind off of
the voluntary parts of the movement, it just

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.690
becomes easier.

00:29:07.690 --> 00:29:13.740
And what you end up finding is that your system
becomes very, very good at doing, forget small

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:19.399
steps or jogging, I'm not going excruciatingly
slow, but for some people it seem really slow.

00:29:19.399 --> 00:29:26.330
But that run or that long-weighted hike accomplishes
the endurance piece.

00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:30.059
It checks off the box of the zone two cardio
requirement.

00:29:30.059 --> 00:29:31.710
Not all of it for the week, but a lot of it.

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:35.640
Tim Ferriss: And for the lay folks out there,
zone two would be, you could have a conversation,

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:36.940
but you really don't want to.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:37.940
Andrew Huberman: That's right.

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:38.940
Tim Ferriss: As Peter might describe.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:39.940
Peter Attia.

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:40.940
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:42.340
And Peter's big on doing long Sunday rucks.

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:45.350
He throws on a rucksack because he's tougher
than me.

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:47.370
Tim Ferriss: Well, you're doing the same thing.

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:49.480
You're just filling it full of water and other
heavy —

00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:51.429
Andrew Huberman: I have a feeling his rucksack
is heavier than mine.

00:29:51.429 --> 00:29:53.399
Peter is, I've trained with Peter.

00:29:53.399 --> 00:29:54.940
Peter likes to push himself.

00:29:54.940 --> 00:30:02.210
These Sunday long, slow jogs or hikes are
really for my mind as much as they are for

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:03.210
my body.

00:30:03.210 --> 00:30:10.770
And I'm convinced that they also carry over
to my ability to endure boring stuff during

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:11.770
the week.

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:15.429
But also it just my ability to work longer
for longer bouts.

00:30:15.429 --> 00:30:18.390
So that's Sunday, also gets me outside a lot.

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:22.320
And oftentimes on Monday, because of the constraints
of the work week, I'm not going to be able

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:23.740
to be outside as much as I would like.

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:25.900
So you get a lot of sun and movement on Sunday.

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:27.340
You feel pretty terrific on Monday.

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:31.360
And let's just earmark what we, or go back
to that earmark earlier, which is that the

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:35.309
state that we're in on Monday has a lot to
do with what we did on Sunday.

00:30:35.309 --> 00:30:39.570
So I'm trying to optimize for Monday in some
ways too, but it's really about endurance.

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:45.890
Then Monday is, the goal for me is to train
my legs, just get a leg workout on Monday.

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:49.570
First of all, I just like the way that sounds
to myself, like legwork workout on Monday,

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:52.679
but it also sets up the work week really nicely.

00:30:52.679 --> 00:30:53.679
Here's why.

00:30:53.679 --> 00:30:56.809
I'm going to train my legs the way that's
always worked best for me for training, which

00:30:56.809 --> 00:31:03.440
is a warmup, and then two to three, maybe
four hard sets, kind of Mike Mentzer or Dorian

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:04.440
Yates.

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:05.909
It's not with four straps and all of that.

00:31:05.909 --> 00:31:12.710
But what we're talking about is warming up
and then doing hard sets that are heavier

00:31:12.710 --> 00:31:14.490
or more repetitions than the last time.

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:19.549
Tim Ferriss: And just so I'm clear, are we
talking about multiple sets of the same exercise,

00:31:19.549 --> 00:31:21.529
single sets of four different exercises?

00:31:21.529 --> 00:31:22.529
What are we talking about?

00:31:22.529 --> 00:31:26.919
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, we're talking about
two to four sets, but usually two to three

00:31:26.919 --> 00:31:29.519
of two exercises per muscle group.

00:31:29.519 --> 00:31:31.100
And I'll explain what that is in a moment.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:34.559
I should mention that the reason we're training
legs is that everyone should train legs.

00:31:34.559 --> 00:31:38.899
So your large muscle groups, I'm trying to
maintain some lower body strength or build

00:31:38.899 --> 00:31:43.029
lower body strength and explosiveness.

00:31:43.029 --> 00:31:47.980
The data that I see on longevity and just
simply ability to perform different sports

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:52.789
and to just feel strong throughout the body
is strongly rooted in the legs.

00:31:52.789 --> 00:31:53.820
So don't skip leg day.

00:31:53.820 --> 00:31:54.820
Tim Ferriss: Legs and hips, folks.

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:55.820
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, exactly.

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:56.820
Tim Ferriss: Feed the wolves.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:59.100
Andrew Huberman: It's kind of funny how glutes
have become the new biceps.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:02.080
This is what I heard, glutes are the new biceps.

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:04.769
Growing up, this was not the case.

00:32:04.769 --> 00:32:09.399
It was like, the '90s,. everyone was like
these waify — I went to a school of waify

00:32:09.399 --> 00:32:11.710
hacky sacker dudes with the flowy hair.

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:12.710
I wasn't one of those.

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.680
All the girls liked those guys, a bunch of
skateboarders, the skinny skateboarders, and

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:17.960
it was the kind of waif era.

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:21.450
I don't know what it is now, it doesn't matter,
but train your legs, folks.

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:24.630
Having strong legs is great and —
Tim Ferriss: Or learn to hacky sack.

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:25.830
Andrew Huberman: Exactly.

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:26.830
Hacky sack.

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:29.190
I'm sure that's a great skill for the mind
for other reasons.

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:31.201
So the '90s are coming back popular in a popular
—

00:32:31.201 --> 00:32:32.201
Tim Ferriss: That's huge.

00:32:32.201 --> 00:32:33.201
Andrew Huberman: Style.

00:32:33.201 --> 00:32:34.201
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:32:34.201 --> 00:32:35.201
You see the youngsters with the Nirvana shirts.

00:32:35.201 --> 00:32:36.201
Andrew Huberman: That's amazing.

00:32:36.201 --> 00:32:37.201
Tim Ferriss: It's all coming around.

00:32:37.201 --> 00:32:38.201
Andrew Huberman: Amazing.

00:32:38.201 --> 00:32:39.201
Tim Ferriss: What was old is once new again.

00:32:39.201 --> 00:32:40.201
Andrew Huberman: Exactly.

00:32:40.201 --> 00:32:41.201
Those were good years.

00:32:41.201 --> 00:32:42.201
So good years, bad clothes.

00:32:42.201 --> 00:32:46.179
So Monday is really about getting that leg
work workout in to make my whole body strong.

00:32:46.179 --> 00:32:47.700
Tim Ferriss: And what exercises do you perform?

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:49.309
Andrew Huberman: It's walk in.

00:32:49.309 --> 00:32:53.870
Oftentimes I'll do calf work because, unlike
you, I need work there.

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:55.889
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I need calf work.

00:32:55.889 --> 00:33:01.550
That was the weak link in the chain for all
of the winter sports I've been doing for the

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:02.550
last six weeks.

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:03.649
Andrew Huberman: I definitely do a lot of
calf work.

00:33:03.649 --> 00:33:06.320
So I'll just walk through it, I am really
big on tib raises.

00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:08.269
I warm up with tib raises.

00:33:08.269 --> 00:33:10.549
Training the front —
Tim Ferriss: Tib raises meaning the tibialis

00:33:10.549 --> 00:33:11.549
anterior?

00:33:11.549 --> 00:33:12.549
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:33:12.549 --> 00:33:14.450
Tim Ferriss: Like dorsiflexion, raising your
toes towards your knee?

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:16.350
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, so this is a huge addition.

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:21.480
My program, I'm a huge fan of Ben Patrick,
Knees Over Toes Guy, as he calls himself on

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:23.080
Instagram.

00:33:23.080 --> 00:33:26.560
I started doing tib work about two years ago,
seriously doing tib work.

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:32.070
So tibialis raises, you can do this also leaning
against a wall at an angle with your back

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:35.279
against the wall and your feet out in front
of you with your heels on the ground and touching

00:33:35.279 --> 00:33:38.710
your toes to the ground, and then lifting
them up for repetitions of 25 to 30.

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:43.870
Or if you can have a tib raise machine, as
they're called, that's great.

00:33:43.870 --> 00:33:45.539
I warm up with tib work.

00:33:45.539 --> 00:33:46.539
Why?

00:33:46.539 --> 00:33:56.409
Training my tibs, as they're called, definitely
makes the calf work more effective, never

00:33:56.409 --> 00:34:01.159
could grow calves or getting my calves strong,
gotten them substantially bigger and stronger

00:34:01.159 --> 00:34:06.250
by training tibs, but more importantly perhaps
helps posture, got rid of my right side sciatica.

00:34:06.250 --> 00:34:14.869
I always had this right side leaning in pain,
and I'm going to get teased for saying this,

00:34:14.869 --> 00:34:17.460
for me, anyway, I can run like a beast now.

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:19.810
No knee pain, no back pain, no shoulder pain.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:22.390
I can just run and run and run.

00:34:22.390 --> 00:34:26.290
So training your tibs turns out to be key
and it turns out it has everything to do with

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:31.710
the, we'll avoid jargon here, bringing your
toe closer to the kneecap as you generate

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:33.649
your stride, not having the floppy feet.

00:34:33.649 --> 00:34:37.859
So if you lay down every night and your toes
are just flopping towards the end of the bed,

00:34:37.859 --> 00:34:38.960
your tibs are weak.

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:40.810
A lot of people with knee issues have weak
tibs.

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:42.119
Tim Ferriss: Well, hold on.

00:34:42.119 --> 00:34:45.750
So if you're in the bed, you've got your sheets
and blankets on top, and your feet are flopping

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:48.710
forward, are you sleeping like a G.I.

00:34:48.710 --> 00:34:51.720
Joe figure with your toes pulled up to your
knees, or what's happening?

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:56.570
Andrew Huberman: No, I just mean that if your
toes are in a state of flaccid relaxation,

00:34:56.570 --> 00:34:57.813
if your feet are flaccid, not good.

00:34:57.813 --> 00:34:59.580
Tim Ferriss: flaccid toes, folks, red light.

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:04.060
Andrew Huberman: A lot of people who run are
smacking their feet against the ground.

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:08.880
Ben cued people to this, tib work is great
for the calves, it's great for the knee, it's

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:11.390
great for the hip, that's all very clear.

00:35:11.390 --> 00:35:16.410
And I think just a lot of people have overtrained
their calves and not balanced it out with

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:19.650
tib work, it would be doing a lot of bicep
working and not a lot of tricep work, or a

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:21.570
lot of quad work and not a lot of hamstring
work.

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:23.020
You have to work both sides of the limb.

00:35:23.020 --> 00:35:26.609
And I think our friend Kelly Starrett would
agree.

00:35:26.609 --> 00:35:28.710
Tib work has changed everything for me.

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:30.770
Posturally, I have no pain any longer.

00:35:30.770 --> 00:35:34.609
Tim Ferriss: I just want to lodge a formal
complaint against Kelly Starrett, people should

00:35:34.609 --> 00:35:38.310
look him up, because he is a large man.

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:39.650
Andrew Huberman: Very.

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:41.440
Tim Ferriss: He's Quadzilla.

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:45.589
He's 230, maybe, former high-level competitive
kayaker.

00:35:45.589 --> 00:35:50.900
I think he celebrated his 40th birthday by
doing a standing back flip, running an ultra,

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:54.230
and something else, and he's a really good
skier.

00:35:54.230 --> 00:35:55.810
And I just want to say it really upsets me.

00:35:55.810 --> 00:35:58.020
That he has no discernible physical weaknesses.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:59.020
It's very irritating.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:00.020
Andrew Huberman: And a nice guy.

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:01.020
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, and a nice guy.

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:02.020
Andrew Huberman: And a nice guy.

00:36:02.020 --> 00:36:03.020
He's very, very strong.

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:04.020
600-pound deadlift.

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:05.020
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he's a strong unit.

00:36:05.020 --> 00:36:06.020
Andrew Huberman: He's a beast.

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:07.020
Tim Ferriss: So tib work.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:08.020
Andrew Huberman: Tib work.

00:36:08.020 --> 00:36:11.020
So I start with tib raises, so it's going
to be a couple warmups, maybe a 12-rep warmup,

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:18.801
an eight-rep warmup, and then I'll do three
sets per tib of anywhere from six to 10 reps.

00:36:18.801 --> 00:36:24.890
Andy Galpin told me, and the literature supports
— people like Brad Schoenfeld and others

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:30.460
have shown that for hypertrophy, for muscle
growth, six to 30 reps, anywhere in there

00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:33.770
can get you hypertrophy if you go to failure
and if you go hard.

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:37.630
I personally like to keep my work resistance
workouts an hour or less.

00:36:37.630 --> 00:36:42.869
I like to train in the more or less the five
to 10 repetition range for strength and for

00:36:42.869 --> 00:36:43.869
hypertrophy.

00:36:43.869 --> 00:36:44.869
And I'm going for a mix of both.

00:36:44.869 --> 00:36:50.349
So I train tibs, then I do calf sled or standing
calf raises, same thing, two to four sets,

00:36:50.349 --> 00:36:52.260
five to eight, maybe 10 repetitions.

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:53.490
Tim Ferriss: Are these sets to failure?

00:36:53.490 --> 00:36:55.530
Andrew Huberman: These are sets to failure.

00:36:55.530 --> 00:37:00.790
And long ago I had gift of learning from the
great Mike Mentzer and these are sets to failure.

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:06.380
I can't budge another micro inch, but I'm
not quaking and I'm not breaking form.

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:11.069
I'm trying to keep everything nice and taught
and rigid because I can't afford an injury

00:37:11.069 --> 00:37:12.069
at this point.

00:37:12.069 --> 00:37:13.619
Not because I'm a competitive athlete —
Tim Ferriss: No flaccid feet.

00:37:13.619 --> 00:37:15.150
Andrew Huberman: No flaccid feet, yeah.

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:20.720
I'm telling you the tib work is a game changer,
and Knees Over Toes Guy, Ben Patrick, is the

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:25.660
one who's been teaching people that, yes,
everyone can dunk, most everyone can dunk.

00:37:25.660 --> 00:37:28.250
He does dunks into back bends and all this
stuff.

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:33.760
And it largely hinges on tib work and quality
posterior chain work, things like Nordic curls.

00:37:33.760 --> 00:37:37.970
So I'm training calves, that takes about 10,
15 minutes total.

00:37:37.970 --> 00:37:40.060
I try and move relatively quickly through
that.

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:43.560
It's only two to three minutes rest, maybe
four if I'm going for a heavy set.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:48.710
Then I'm weaker in the hamstrings than I am
in my quads so I do two warmups and then two

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:54.000
to four working sets of lying leg curls, pretty
standard stuff, and then I go to —

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:55.930
Tim Ferriss: Lying leg curls meaning on a
machine?

00:37:55.930 --> 00:37:56.930
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, on a machine.

00:37:56.930 --> 00:37:57.930
Tim Ferriss: Not reverse hamstring?

00:37:57.930 --> 00:37:58.930
Andrew Huberman: I have a Nautilus machine
or something.

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:04.540
Not seated, doesn't work for me, just lying
leg curls, and not the hoist machines that

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.000
move with you to make it easier.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.400
No.

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:12.569
And maybe the occasional forced rep if somebody's
there to help me.

00:38:12.569 --> 00:38:17.720
Then I go do two to four sets, but typically
three, of glute-hammer raises, which is an

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:19.690
incredible exercise.

00:38:19.690 --> 00:38:24.230
The equipment isn't in every gym, but I'm
doing about three or four sets of glute-ham

00:38:24.230 --> 00:38:26.400
raises going slow.

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:29.770
And this is basically if you were going to
do a deadlift, everyone knows what a deadlift

00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:33.030
is, but now take the ground and rotate it
90 degrees and make it the wall.

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:34.880
That's what a glute-ham raise really is.

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:38.000
It allows you to do a deadlift, but then at
the top do a leg curl.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:43.079
So if you think about it that way, you just
tilted the ground, you just rotated it counterclockwise

00:38:43.079 --> 00:38:44.079
90 degrees.

00:38:44.079 --> 00:38:45.079
Tim Ferriss: We'll get a link to a YouTube,
folks.

00:38:45.079 --> 00:38:46.079
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, exactly.

00:38:46.079 --> 00:38:48.119
Glute-ham raise is a great lower back, so
entire posterior chain.

00:38:48.119 --> 00:38:52.670
So then I'm done with calves and hamstrings,
and then I'll do two or three sets of leg

00:38:52.670 --> 00:38:56.240
extensions, so maybe a warmup and then two
or three sets of working leg extensions, which

00:38:56.240 --> 00:38:59.750
for whatever reasons are incredibly painful,
I hate them, but they work to isolate the

00:38:59.750 --> 00:39:05.470
quads, and then two or three sets of working
hack squats after a warmup hack.

00:39:05.470 --> 00:39:08.430
Hack squats, heavy, heavy, heavy, heavy.

00:39:08.430 --> 00:39:09.470
And then I'm done.

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:10.530
I'm out.

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:11.530
Monday's done.

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:13.990
Now this is, again, going back to the overall
theme, the idea is to —

00:39:13.990 --> 00:39:17.819
Tim Ferriss: Why hack squats specifically
versus other forms of squats?

00:39:17.819 --> 00:39:22.670
Andrew Huberman: Back squats, for me, I always
got a internal hip pain, I had every squat

00:39:22.670 --> 00:39:26.420
coach in the world tell me how to do it better,
even tell me I was doing it, and then I end

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:30.880
up with the same thing, and I don't really
care if I can squat X amount of weight.

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:31.880
I'm doing it for strength.

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:33.069
Tim Ferriss: You're not doing it for a power
lifting competition?

00:39:33.069 --> 00:39:34.811
Andrew Huberman: No, doing it for strength
and aesthetics.

00:39:34.811 --> 00:39:37.610
Aesthetics just because it's some balance.

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:41.140
I'm not trying to get huge legs, but I'm 6.1,
220.

00:39:41.140 --> 00:39:43.290
I sit more or less right there all the time.

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:47.089
My body fat goes up and down, might gain five
pounds or lose five pounds, but I'm hovering

00:39:47.089 --> 00:39:48.089
around there.

00:39:48.089 --> 00:39:50.600
I don't have any interest in being much larger
or much smaller.

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:54.410
I want to keep my strength, maybe build some
as I get older.

00:39:54.410 --> 00:39:58.210
And so hack squats allow me to put a lot of
weight on there and keep my nice right angle

00:39:58.210 --> 00:39:59.630
between the hip and my back.

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:02.829
Tim Ferriss: Can you describe for folks, just
in case they're going to go searching for

00:40:02.829 --> 00:40:05.220
this, what is the visual of a hack squat?

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:08.891
Andrew Huberman: Hack squat, you're sitting
back, you're back is against a sled, you're

00:40:08.891 --> 00:40:13.160
holding the handles up near your ears, and
then you're squatting down, your feet are

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:19.180
on a 45-degree or, ideally if you can find
it, a 30-degree platform below you.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:21.849
So it's not a leg press, it's a hack squat.

00:40:21.849 --> 00:40:24.800
Now for people that don't have access to these,
and unfortunately a lot of gyms don't have

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:31.030
them anymore or just don't keep them around
for whatever reason, weighted sissy squats,

00:40:31.030 --> 00:40:34.600
as they're sometimes called, can work where
you're holding onto a plate and you're squatting

00:40:34.600 --> 00:40:36.550
down while holding onto a pole.

00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:40.170
Tim Ferriss: It's deceptively hard to do if
you do it under control and with good technique.

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:42.230
Andrew Huberman: And if you're doing your
tib work, you don't have to —

00:40:42.230 --> 00:40:46.950
Tim Ferriss: Great way to stretch your quads
in ski boots too, if you're going up in —

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:52.320
Andrew Huberman: Recently our podcast team
took a little ski trip, I was snowboarding,

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:54.980
and it's been a long time since I've been
on a snowboard, but you start to feel how

00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:58.660
many of these movements translate, as you've
pointed out.

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:02.910
And I should say that the sissy squats, a
lot of people think you can't bring your knee

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:04.010
out over your toe.

00:41:04.010 --> 00:41:08.140
And this is what Ben Patrick has really been
trying to impress on people, look at skateboarders

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:13.760
land, their knees go out over their toes,
a foot beyond their toes.

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:16.260
Look at parkour, gymnasts —
Tim Ferriss: Olympic weight weightlifters.

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:19.890
Andrew Huberman: This whole knee can't pass
the toe thing is just silly.

00:41:19.890 --> 00:41:24.050
So you can feel very comfortable and very
strong at the bottom of one of these sissy

00:41:24.050 --> 00:41:25.569
squats or hack squats that way.

00:41:25.569 --> 00:41:29.829
And so the whole purpose of that Monday workout,
it's the opposite of what I'm doing on Sunday,

00:41:29.829 --> 00:41:34.190
it's get stronger, maintain some size, but
really get stronger in the legs.

00:41:34.190 --> 00:41:37.320
Tim Ferriss: And just for people listening
who are like, "For fuck's sake, it's going

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:41.990
to take us six hours to get to Saturday,"
part of the reason that I want to dig into

00:41:41.990 --> 00:41:44.250
this day specifically is because it's so neglected.

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:52.109
People do not tend to exercise with a focus
on their legs, but the direct and collateral

00:41:52.109 --> 00:41:55.960
benefits are so numerous that I want to just
drill into this.

00:41:55.960 --> 00:42:00.460
Andrew Huberman: When you look at the literature
on cognitive improvements from resistance

00:42:00.460 --> 00:42:05.050
training, it's not from bicep curls, it's
not from bench presses.

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:09.500
I suppose it could be from things like dips,
which are like a upper body squat of sorts,

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:14.880
especially if they're weighted or with rings
or something, but training the legs is key.

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:17.240
And so, as I said before, there are two goals.

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:22.569
One is to get the legs stronger, the other
is that I'm trying to create a systemic anabolic

00:42:22.569 --> 00:42:23.569
effect on the body.

00:42:23.569 --> 00:42:27.180
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I was just about to say,
if you want to lose body fat also, the systemic

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:32.260
endocrine/anabolic effect from doing this
lower body exercise is significant.

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:34.160
Andrew Huberman: It's real work.

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:39.210
And resting long between the sets, especially
the hamstring and quad work, four or five

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:43.569
minutes, so you start to feel lazy, but you're
going all out, you're breathing really hard

00:42:43.569 --> 00:42:46.329
after the set, sometimes you feel like you're
going to pass out.

00:42:46.329 --> 00:42:49.960
I haven't puked from a leg workout yet, which
people tell me means I haven't really trained

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:53.559
hard, but I just say that I just don't have
a weak stomach like the rest of them.

00:42:53.559 --> 00:42:58.490
Tim Ferriss: Look, as Kelly Starrett would
say, "20-rep squats work just great and you'll

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:02.910
puke into a bucket, but you're not going to
be able to do much else for the next week

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:05.160
if you do them really intensely."

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:08.430
It's going to hurt you every time you sit
on a toilet seat, so you're not going to be

00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:11.030
doing a whole lot of basketball very well.

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:15.990
Andrew Huberman: I want strong legs, I want
a strong body, I want a body that can accomplish

00:43:15.990 --> 00:43:17.350
endurance, and I want the cognitive effects.

00:43:17.350 --> 00:43:20.390
So you get that systemic anabolic effect.

00:43:20.390 --> 00:43:24.770
There's another practical reason for doing
this on Monday, which is sometimes I might

00:43:24.770 --> 00:43:28.190
not train again until Thursday.

00:43:28.190 --> 00:43:34.500
And if you've trained your legs properly,
you can know that you initiated a number of

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:36.349
good processes in your brain and body.

00:43:36.349 --> 00:43:37.830
Tim Ferriss: Made a down payment for the week.

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:38.940
Andrew Huberman: You made a bit of a down
payment.

00:43:38.940 --> 00:43:42.060
Also, if you think about the neural circuits
involved in generating the kinds of movements

00:43:42.060 --> 00:43:45.829
I just described for the Monday workout, they're
fundamentally different than the kinds of

00:43:45.829 --> 00:43:48.900
movements and neural circuits required for
generating movements.

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:52.670
So the Sunday long workout, they're both legs,
you're running on your legs or walking your

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:54.849
legs, of course, but very, very different.

00:43:54.849 --> 00:43:57.130
Different muscle fiber types, different motor
neurons involved.

00:43:57.130 --> 00:43:58.260
Tim Ferriss: Different range of motion too.

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:00.710
Andrew Huberman: Different range of motion
and different mindset required.

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:06.211
And keep in mind the entire leg workout takes
10 minutes of warm up and about 55.0 minutes

00:44:06.211 --> 00:44:07.310
of training.

00:44:07.310 --> 00:44:09.010
Tuesday is very different.

00:44:09.010 --> 00:44:11.859
Oh, and by the way, Monday is a very heavy
workday for me.

00:44:11.859 --> 00:44:16.250
Typically, we launch podcasts, I do all my
own social media, so I post the assets, I

00:44:16.250 --> 00:44:21.000
really like doing that, respond to comments,
dealing with grants, dealing with papers.

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:24.970
So Monday is heavy work day, so getting the
leg workout done early on Monday is really

00:44:24.970 --> 00:44:26.099
key for me.

00:44:26.099 --> 00:44:31.110
These days, I don't ever really do resistance
training past 11:00 a.m. and ideally earlier

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:32.110
in the day.

00:44:32.110 --> 00:44:35.570
And we won't go into whether or not it's fasted
or fed or other because I've covered that.

00:44:35.570 --> 00:44:40.180
I have a toolkit where I list out some of
that and how different processes layer together,

00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:41.780
and I can link to that.

00:44:41.780 --> 00:44:47.500
Tim Ferriss: I would also say, not to interject,
but I will, if people are like, "But wait,

00:44:47.500 --> 00:44:51.770
I can't start until I know if it's fasted,
or non-fasted, or if I should be swinging

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:54.050
the dead cat over my head on Tuesday or Wednesday
night."

00:44:54.050 --> 00:44:55.290
It's just get started.

00:44:55.290 --> 00:44:56.670
You've got plenty to get started.

00:44:56.670 --> 00:45:00.010
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I do it fasted, but
I drink caffeine first, and water.

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:02.740
And listen, I usually eat a bit more on the
weekend.

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:03.740
This is great.

00:45:03.740 --> 00:45:06.880
Sunday night you're putting in your fuel sources
for your Monday workout.

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:08.280
There are all sorts of ways this layers together.

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:09.280
Tim Ferriss: Cookies.

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:10.280
Andrew Huberman: I'm not a cookie guy.

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:11.280
Tim Ferriss: I'm just kidding.

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:14.599
Andrew Huberman: For me it's like pizza is
the — I do love pizza.

00:45:14.599 --> 00:45:17.030
No cheat days anymore for me however, I haven't
done one in a while.

00:45:17.030 --> 00:45:20.660
I'm actually thinking of going back on The
4-Hour Body, just try it and do it.

00:45:20.660 --> 00:45:21.660
It's got to still work.

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:28.290
Tim Ferriss: I said cookies, I'll make a very
embarrassing admission, which is I am going

00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:36.380
to be going back onto strict slow carb, and
so I had these incredible cookies last night.

00:45:36.380 --> 00:45:39.440
We're not going to spend a lot of time on
this because we do need to get to Tuesday,

00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:43.390
but it was my last hurrah before locking down
the fort.

00:45:43.390 --> 00:45:47.120
So yes, I'm getting back on —
Andrew Huberman: Let me know, I'll start with

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:48.120
you.

00:45:48.120 --> 00:45:49.120
Lots of stories about cheat days.

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:54.109
My ex-girlfriend, we used to do the cheat
days together, and at one point, I'll blame

00:45:54.109 --> 00:45:59.180
myself, we were in couple's therapy and they
were asking, "So describe a week for yourself."

00:45:59.180 --> 00:46:03.319
And she's like, "Well, then on Sunday we're
eating eight croissants."

00:46:03.319 --> 00:46:09.400
And this therapist was looking at us, laughing
so hard, trying not to laugh like, "What in

00:46:09.400 --> 00:46:11.369
the world is this?"

00:46:11.369 --> 00:46:12.500
But we had a good time with it.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:17.880
She was one of these mutants that could just
eat anything, drink anything, and feel fine

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:18.880
the next day, and never put on pounds.

00:46:18.880 --> 00:46:19.880
Tim Ferriss: I know the type.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:24.130
Andrew Huberman: So the Tuesday is very different.

00:46:24.130 --> 00:46:28.420
Tuesday, I don't want to call it a recovery
day, but Tuesday I'm doing something really

00:46:28.420 --> 00:46:29.420
different.

00:46:29.420 --> 00:46:35.720
First of all, my legs need recovery, so what
I'll do is substantial amounts of deliberate

00:46:35.720 --> 00:46:37.750
heat exposure and deliberate cold exposure.

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:40.950
Yes, I do cold showers in the morning first
thing nowadays.

00:46:40.950 --> 00:46:47.700
Yes, it is a bad idea to do cold water immersion
after hypertrophy training.

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:52.970
So just for the record, you don't want to
get into an ice bath in the six hours after

00:46:52.970 --> 00:46:55.160
a hypertrophy training because it can blunt
the hypertrophy.

00:46:55.160 --> 00:46:59.640
It blocks the inflammation, which is exactly
what you want to trigger the adaptation of

00:46:59.640 --> 00:47:00.640
hypertrophy.

00:47:00.640 --> 00:47:06.670
But Tuesday is really about getting the maximum
effect of heat exposure and cold exposure.

00:47:06.670 --> 00:47:10.650
I've done multiple episodes on each one of
those, I learned about deliberate cold exposure

00:47:10.650 --> 00:47:13.369
from you, so thank you, Tim.

00:47:13.369 --> 00:47:19.559
I do a protocol which is pretty intense, designed
to amplify growth hormone, stimulate a bunch

00:47:19.559 --> 00:47:26.050
of positive, mood-promoting hormones that
last not just days but weeks, say the literature,

00:47:26.050 --> 00:47:27.420
and just get better at it.

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:29.760
So I'll do 20 minutes of sauna, hot sauna.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:30.760
Tim Ferriss: What does hot mean?

00:47:30.760 --> 00:47:33.370
Andrew Huberman: I'm up to 260 now.

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:34.380
But I worked up to that.

00:47:34.380 --> 00:47:38.410
You can check out the Banya, I'll give a plug
to these folks, they're Spa 88 on Wall Street

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:39.410
in New York.

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:40.510
They are amazing.

00:47:40.510 --> 00:47:41.829
They are incredible.

00:47:41.829 --> 00:47:43.680
They have a hot Russian sauna.

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:44.680
Tim Ferriss: They have very hot sauna.

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:47.110
Andrew Huberman: And Archimedes Banya in San
Francisco is great.

00:47:47.110 --> 00:47:49.900
A couple of notes about that one, it's clothing
optional.

00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:51.850
I wear my swim trunks.

00:47:51.850 --> 00:47:52.850
Tim Ferriss: Boo!

00:47:52.850 --> 00:47:56.559
How are you going to show off those glutes
you're working so hard on?

00:47:56.559 --> 00:47:59.940
Andrew Huberman: But it is, so just know what
you're getting into, it's co-ed and clothing

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:00.940
optional.

00:48:00.940 --> 00:48:03.619
And it is in Hunters Point, Bayview, which
is a rough neighborhood.

00:48:03.619 --> 00:48:05.660
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I wouldn't go for a jog.

00:48:05.660 --> 00:48:07.840
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, don't leave anything
in your car, but don't do that anywhere in

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:09.600
San Francisco now.

00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:15.130
So 20 minutes in the sauna, very hot, three
to five minutes in the cold plunge up to the

00:48:15.130 --> 00:48:18.950
neck, back into the sauna for 20 minutes,
back into the cold plunge for three to five

00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:23.010
minutes, back into the sauna for 20 minutes,
back into the coal plunge for three to five

00:48:23.010 --> 00:48:24.160
minutes.

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:33.569
It's work, but it's amazing in the sense that
you recover very well from the leg day, you

00:48:33.569 --> 00:48:37.619
generate all the hormone neurotransmitter
type of adaptations that one wants, and you

00:48:37.619 --> 00:48:40.280
get very, very good at tolerating heat and
cold.

00:48:40.280 --> 00:48:43.410
And I should mention, during these times,
if there isn't someone else there, I'm listening

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:48.990
to books, I'm thinking, I'm putting this work
and time to use, so there's multiple things

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:49.990
going on there.

00:48:49.990 --> 00:48:52.809
Then Wednesday, I do one of two things.

00:48:52.809 --> 00:48:58.369
I'm either going to do a shorter duration
than Sunday cardiovascular training workout,

00:48:58.369 --> 00:49:04.430
so I'm thinking about five minutes of warmup
and then about 25 to 30 minutes of usually

00:49:04.430 --> 00:49:08.391
running for me, where I'm just trying to get
out and cover as much distance as I can at

00:49:08.391 --> 00:49:12.430
a fast clip but steady, so I'm not sprint,
stop, sprint, stop.

00:49:12.430 --> 00:49:13.660
That's typically what I'll do on Wednesday.

00:49:13.660 --> 00:49:18.630
Although, if my legs are still a little bit
sore, and here the body builders are just

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:23.290
going to go, "Ugh," they're going to scoff,
I train what I call torso.

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:24.290
What do I do?

00:49:24.290 --> 00:49:29.050
I try to get pushing through the chest and
shoulders, I'm trying to pull for the back.

00:49:29.050 --> 00:49:35.079
I already got my lower back with the glute-ham
raises, so what I tend to do is overhead shoulder

00:49:35.079 --> 00:49:39.280
presses after a warmup, two to three sets,
working sets, or maybe four.

00:49:39.280 --> 00:49:44.901
I like ring dips and dips these days, those
are hard for me, but I do two or three work

00:49:44.901 --> 00:49:47.930
sets of those, so chest, shoulders.

00:49:47.930 --> 00:49:52.970
And I'm going to upset some people here, but
I don't tend to train back every week, I do

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:56.809
it every other week because just I have some
genetic abnormality where those grow really

00:49:56.809 --> 00:49:59.270
easily and I can throw proportions off really
quickly.

00:49:59.270 --> 00:50:05.970
But I might do three or four sets of max rep
chins slow, with slow, eccentric movements,

00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:06.970
the lowering as well.

00:50:06.970 --> 00:50:10.059
Tim Ferriss: Now, slow eccentric, four seconds,
10 seconds, what are we talking about?

00:50:10.059 --> 00:50:14.510
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, usually about four
or five seconds, and then slowly pulling up,

00:50:14.510 --> 00:50:15.810
contracting whatever muscle group.

00:50:15.810 --> 00:50:19.590
So all of the movements are done trying to
move as much weight as possible as quickly

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:25.090
as possible on the concentric phase, and then
lowering it anywhere from two to four seconds,

00:50:25.090 --> 00:50:26.950
loading it like a spring and then trying to
explode.

00:50:26.950 --> 00:50:30.210
I want strength and I want explosiveness,
and some hypertrophy sneaks in because I'm

00:50:30.210 --> 00:50:34.190
working in that five to eight rep range.

00:50:34.190 --> 00:50:39.500
So that's what I call torso because it's chest,
shoulders, back, every other week I'll throw

00:50:39.500 --> 00:50:40.760
in those chins.

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:44.670
And I think everyone has a muscle group like
this where if they train it just grows like

00:50:44.670 --> 00:50:49.810
a weed, but I want to keep proportions right,
strength proportions as well.

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:52.000
And I do train my neck that day as well.

00:50:52.000 --> 00:50:53.559
I know you wrestled.

00:50:53.559 --> 00:50:58.440
I can tell very easily looking at somebody
whether or not they need neck work or not,

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:04.829
if your neck comes down where your jaw is,
you don't need to do a lot of neck work, you

00:51:04.829 --> 00:51:08.490
don't look like a head placed on top of a
little neck.

00:51:08.490 --> 00:51:12.109
Laird Hamilton's neck is out to the lobes
of his ears, genuinely.

00:51:12.109 --> 00:51:15.119
Danny Way, great skateboarder too, he trains
his neck quite a lot.

00:51:15.119 --> 00:51:17.280
He broke his neck surfing years ago.

00:51:17.280 --> 00:51:20.690
So having a strong neck, why is that so important?

00:51:20.690 --> 00:51:26.099
Well, it's important because you want a strong
spine and it's the upper portion of your spine.

00:51:26.099 --> 00:51:30.140
The other thing that I notice that it does
is it completely changes my psychology to

00:51:30.140 --> 00:51:31.140
train my neck.

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:35.010
I just naturally stand more upright, I find
it easier to look people in the eye, it's

00:51:35.010 --> 00:51:38.700
not hard for me to look people directly in
the eye when I speak to them, but I find my

00:51:38.700 --> 00:51:42.990
posture and presence is just better in a chair
or standing when I train my neck.

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:46.950
And I think it's because my head, let's just
use the word again, it's not flaccid, falling

00:51:46.950 --> 00:51:51.460
of the chin towards the chest, that word just
freaks everybody out.

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:56.329
You want flaccid feet, you don't want a flaccid
neck, so neck work is very important.

00:51:56.329 --> 00:51:59.890
Tim Ferriss: How do you work your neck?

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:03.750
I've been thinking about it for decades, I
bought a four-way neck machine, and it's actually,

00:52:03.750 --> 00:52:05.329
I got it on —
Andrew Huberman: Wait, you got the full four-way

00:52:05.329 --> 00:52:06.329
neck machine?

00:52:06.329 --> 00:52:11.239
Tim Ferriss: I was going to get a Hammer or
one of these great, giant contraptions.

00:52:11.239 --> 00:52:15.010
And look, do your homework folks, because
you can hurt yourself on these things if you

00:52:15.010 --> 00:52:16.970
get too aggressive too quickly.

00:52:16.970 --> 00:52:20.069
I bought a four-way neck machine on Amazon
for 350 bucks.

00:52:20.069 --> 00:52:21.390
I was like, "You know what?

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:24.650
Let me try this before spending five grand."

00:52:24.650 --> 00:52:26.150
Works great.

00:52:26.150 --> 00:52:27.800
Nothing fancy, but it works.

00:52:27.800 --> 00:52:28.950
Andrew Huberman: You have great proportions.

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:30.150
Why do I say this?

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:35.060
It's not just about aesthetics, it's about,
in general, balanced proportions are synonymous

00:52:35.060 --> 00:52:38.630
with balanced strength, which is synonymous
with not getting injured.

00:52:38.630 --> 00:52:42.730
One of the things that looks ridiculous and
frankly is ridiculous, you see these guys

00:52:42.730 --> 00:52:49.280
with big delts, wide shoulders, long clavicles,
and their head is placed on this little neck

00:52:49.280 --> 00:52:50.510
—
Tim Ferriss: It's just a popsicle on a stick.

00:52:50.510 --> 00:52:54.270
Andrew Huberman: And they look especially
ridiculous, there's no other word for it,

00:52:54.270 --> 00:53:00.180
in street clothes, it looks mutant, and not
in the good sense.

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:02.370
So strong neck is great.

00:53:02.370 --> 00:53:05.560
Strong neck has helped me also avoid injuries
in a number of sports.

00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:10.270
It's life insurance if you do any kind of
martial art, or you drive a car.

00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:14.410
I've been rear-ended in a car and felt fine,
a little bit of neck soreness, but train the

00:53:14.410 --> 00:53:16.349
upper spine and lower spine.

00:53:16.349 --> 00:53:17.741
So I do some neck work at the end.

00:53:17.741 --> 00:53:22.020
And the way I do it is take a plate, wrap
it in a towel so you don't end up with an

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:29.299
imprint of the weight number on your forehead,
and I usually lie on my side and I'll do somewhere

00:53:29.299 --> 00:53:31.599
in the 10- to 15-repetition range.

00:53:31.599 --> 00:53:34.430
I'll usually do one light warmup and then
three work sets.

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:35.660
Tim Ferriss: How do you hold it on your head?

00:53:35.660 --> 00:53:38.349
Andrew Huberman: You just hold it with your
hand on your side.

00:53:38.349 --> 00:53:39.900
It also works your oblique somewhat.

00:53:39.900 --> 00:53:41.121
Tim Ferriss: So you're on a bench or on the
ground?

00:53:41.121 --> 00:53:42.121
Andrew Huberman: On a bench.

00:53:42.121 --> 00:53:45.490
You have to be careful, especially getting
into and out of the movement, that's how you

00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:47.020
often get injured.

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:50.089
And then I'll lie on my stomach, and this
is probably the most important one, and I'll

00:53:50.089 --> 00:53:56.760
put the plate behind my head, again, wrapped
in a towel, and do head raises.

00:53:56.760 --> 00:54:02.070
You're trying to get your head sitting on
top of your shoulders as it's supposed to,

00:54:02.070 --> 00:54:04.720
because everyone now is bent over in this
C-shape.

00:54:04.720 --> 00:54:09.000
And no one's nerdy enough, except my good
friend Brian Mackenzie, who cares enough about

00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:10.440
his posture — shout out to Brian.

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:13.180
He does amazing work in breath work and human
performance.

00:54:13.180 --> 00:54:15.380
When he texts, he uses his phone up near his
eyes.

00:54:15.380 --> 00:54:16.380
Tim Ferriss: I've seen that.

00:54:16.380 --> 00:54:17.380
Andrew Huberman: It's so good.

00:54:17.380 --> 00:54:18.440
He's like, duh, duh, duh.

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:20.849
No one else does that, Brian.

00:54:20.849 --> 00:54:24.380
So the neck work comes at the end, but is
absolutely critical.

00:54:24.380 --> 00:54:29.760
And whenever I don't do it for a week or two
out of laziness or just some other reason,

00:54:29.760 --> 00:54:33.250
I start getting postural things you start
noticing elsewhere in your body.

00:54:33.250 --> 00:54:38.020
Tim Ferriss: I find, I don't think it's placebo
because it's not something that I expect,

00:54:38.020 --> 00:54:48.680
or maybe it is now, but not initially, fewer
headaches, just better circulation and better

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:50.030
mobility also.

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:54.329
Especially if you're spending a lot of time
in front of a computer.

00:54:54.329 --> 00:54:59.830
My rotation, even though I'm not actively
training with resistance, the rotational aspect,

00:54:59.830 --> 00:55:05.619
left, right, turning your head, the mobility
of the neck in all dimensions and the movement

00:55:05.619 --> 00:55:08.549
of the head seems to improve as well for me.

00:55:08.549 --> 00:55:12.740
And for folks, just one option I want to throw
out there, if you train with someone or can

00:55:12.740 --> 00:55:16.230
train with someone, manual resistance —
Andrew Huberman: With a towel, some will say.

00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:20.900
Tim Ferriss: With a towel or just pressure
on the head, and doing it really slowly, super

00:55:20.900 --> 00:55:26.250
slow style, 10 seconds, 10 seconds is very
effective also.

00:55:26.250 --> 00:55:29.480
Andrew Huberman: I can't overstate the importance
of neck training.

00:55:29.480 --> 00:55:37.050
Now for women who often don't want the aesthetic
of a larger neck, just use, I would say, lighter

00:55:37.050 --> 00:55:39.410
weights or hand-based resistance.

00:55:39.410 --> 00:55:42.579
You still want that strength and balance.

00:55:42.579 --> 00:55:46.200
Some women might want a big neck, but, in
general, the two things that can — and you

00:55:46.200 --> 00:55:49.020
put some of this in The 4-Hour Body.

00:55:49.020 --> 00:55:52.440
Tim Ferriss: Throw a wig on Laird Hamilton,
that's my type.

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:54.609
I'm just putting it out there, ladies.

00:55:54.609 --> 00:56:00.040
Andrew Huberman: In terms of aesthetics, the
two things that I think can really throw off

00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:05.270
the most commonly desired female aesthetic
by women from what they tell me, because I've

00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:10.549
trained with a number of women, is excessive
trap work, upper trap work cause rounded shoulders

00:56:10.549 --> 00:56:12.100
and then larger neck.

00:56:12.100 --> 00:56:16.220
Some people might want that, but in general
that tends to be avoided.

00:56:16.220 --> 00:56:23.900
For guys, I think that neck work, it's so
essential, and for women it's essential for

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:27.810
a strength and protection of the joints reason.

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:32.750
I will also say this, and anyone who wants
to challenge me on this will do it accordingly,

00:56:32.750 --> 00:56:35.870
it actually increases your confidence.

00:56:35.870 --> 00:56:39.819
And the reason is when you're upright, you
embody a different stance.

00:56:39.819 --> 00:56:44.230
I'm a big believer that the physical stances
we embody have everything to do with the psychological

00:56:44.230 --> 00:56:46.070
stances we embody and vice versa.

00:56:46.070 --> 00:56:49.940
So it's not just about standing up straight,
being able to stand up straight, look people

00:56:49.940 --> 00:56:54.540
in the eye, it's something that is assisted
by actually having a head that isn't flopping

00:56:54.540 --> 00:56:56.829
forward all the time.

00:56:56.829 --> 00:56:59.750
So this is a real thing from a number of standpoints.

00:56:59.750 --> 00:57:05.380
So if, for whatever reason, I do the cardio
workout that I mentioned, the 25 or 30 minutes

00:57:05.380 --> 00:57:09.270
of running, that's what I do, but some people
might do it on the AssaultBike or something

00:57:09.270 --> 00:57:13.460
like that, or cycling, or rower, on the Wednesday.

00:57:13.460 --> 00:57:18.119
Then I'll do that weight workout I just described,
torso, on Thursday, or I'll swap them.

00:57:18.119 --> 00:57:21.900
So there's some flexibility built into the
week, travel, et cetera.

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:24.960
By the way, the cardiovascular workouts can
be done in the morning or the evening, but

00:57:24.960 --> 00:57:28.650
I always prefer to do them in the morning
and just get it done with.

00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:34.310
Then Friday is a really important day because
Friday is the day that I do a short workout,

00:57:34.310 --> 00:57:39.660
usually only about three minutes of warmup
and about 12 minutes of training, and the

00:57:39.660 --> 00:57:44.369
goal is to get my heart rate as high as I
possibly can, I learned this from Andy Galpin.

00:57:44.369 --> 00:57:48.329
Just increasing VO2 max, getting those really
fastuous muscle fibers.

00:57:48.329 --> 00:57:52.619
My favorite way to do this is I'll get on
the AssaultBike, so the ones with the handles

00:57:52.619 --> 00:57:56.280
with the fan, which is not designed to keep
you cool, it's designed to create resistance,

00:57:56.280 --> 00:58:00.700
folks, and do 20 second on sprint, 10 second
rest, 20 second on, 10 second rest.

00:58:00.700 --> 00:58:01.700
Tim Ferriss: Tabata style.

00:58:01.700 --> 00:58:04.330
Andrew Huberman: Tabata type thing for six
to eight rounds.

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:08.670
And then what I like to do is take a band
and tie it to something like a chin-up bar

00:58:08.670 --> 00:58:13.760
or something, and I'll squat down and jump,
and I'll do it as high jumping as I possibly

00:58:13.760 --> 00:58:15.630
can, but I actually control the eccentric.

00:58:15.630 --> 00:58:18.079
I'm holding the band as I come down.

00:58:18.079 --> 00:58:24.410
And so I learned from Peter Attia, I've learned
from Andy Galpin that our ability to jump

00:58:24.410 --> 00:58:28.470
and land is strongly correlated with physical
longevity.

00:58:28.470 --> 00:58:29.700
Or you could do broad jump.

00:58:29.700 --> 00:58:33.359
So you could do a bunch of broad jumps, you
could do some hit workout, and then I'm just

00:58:33.359 --> 00:58:36.640
showered and out the door to go do my work
or to work.

00:58:36.640 --> 00:58:38.000
So it's a really short workout.

00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:44.049
And then Saturday is the fun one because I
still enjoy this, I'll go into the gym in

00:58:44.049 --> 00:58:51.720
the morning, usually it's mid-morning, and
I'll do small muscle groups, biceps, triceps,

00:58:51.720 --> 00:58:52.839
rear delts.

00:58:52.839 --> 00:58:57.329
I'll do another tip and calf workout, a little
bit lighter than the one on leg day because

00:58:57.329 --> 00:59:01.160
leg day is coming in two days, and the next
day I'm doing that long hike.

00:59:01.160 --> 00:59:05.690
So that day is really to round out the smaller
muscle groups that need work.

00:59:05.690 --> 00:59:09.480
And I have a short torso, long arms, and so
torso muscles grow very easily for me, get

00:59:09.480 --> 00:59:12.890
stronger easily, long arms, but they require
a little bit more work, so I like to do a

00:59:12.890 --> 00:59:14.420
dedicated day for that.

00:59:14.420 --> 00:59:20.020
Same way, warmup plus two to four work sets
of two to three exercises.

00:59:20.020 --> 00:59:24.560
I always include dips at some point during
the week, bench dips, tricep extensions with

00:59:24.560 --> 00:59:29.390
cables, basic stuff, preacher curls, incline
curls, so very basic stuff.

00:59:29.390 --> 00:59:33.360
But I just want to backtrack one step because
I failed to say that Friday the idea is to

00:59:33.360 --> 00:59:34.819
get that VO2 max up.

00:59:34.819 --> 00:59:35.819
But guess what?

00:59:35.819 --> 00:59:38.369
It's also designed to indirectly hit the legs.

00:59:38.369 --> 00:59:41.200
We hit legs on Monday, and they've recovered.

00:59:41.200 --> 00:59:45.720
We now know that protein synthesis maximizes
after these training workouts at about 48

00:59:45.720 --> 00:59:47.220
hours and then starts to taper off.

00:59:47.220 --> 00:59:50.660
Now, you read that, you hear that a lot, especially
on social media, and people think you have

00:59:50.660 --> 00:59:52.750
to hit a muscle group every 48 hours.

00:59:52.750 --> 00:59:56.799
But no, you hold onto the protein synthesis,
you generate it for another couple of days,

00:59:56.799 --> 01:00:01.579
so that Friday sprint on the bike workout
or sprint on a field workout and jumping is

01:00:01.579 --> 01:00:06.210
indirectly targeting the quads and calves
and hamstrings.

01:00:06.210 --> 01:00:08.940
And so you're keeping them online for hypertrophy.

01:00:08.940 --> 01:00:12.351
So when you hear all this, you might think,
gosh, that's a lot of working out, but what

01:00:12.351 --> 01:00:17.660
we're really talking about is a long hike
with friends or family on Sunday or by oneself,

01:00:17.660 --> 01:00:19.970
90 minutes to three hours.

01:00:19.970 --> 01:00:24.359
You're talking about an hour workout on Monday
morning, you're talking about sitting in the

01:00:24.359 --> 01:00:29.250
sauna and cold on Tuesday, you're talking
about running for 20 or 30 minutes on Wednesday,

01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:34.740
you're talking about doing some dips and overhead
presses, maybe some chins and a little bit

01:00:34.740 --> 01:00:41.380
of neck work on Thursday, you're talking about
doing a 12-minute workout on Friday, and you're

01:00:41.380 --> 01:00:45.731
talking about going to the gym for an hour,
hour and 15 minutes, more casual — I don't

01:00:45.731 --> 01:00:48.329
want to call it what it is, but you could
call it a vanity workout.

01:00:48.329 --> 01:00:53.880
I called it that to Joe Rogan and he was like,
"That's ridiculous, bicep is key muscle group."

01:00:53.880 --> 01:00:55.190
And I was like, "Give me an example."

01:00:55.190 --> 01:00:58.130
And he's like, "Well, when you're grappling
and you're about to choke somebody out."

01:00:58.130 --> 01:00:59.710
And I was like, "Okay, well, that's you."

01:00:59.710 --> 01:01:01.359
But he's absolutely right.

01:01:01.359 --> 01:01:06.359
Actually, I chipped my tooth really hard once,
I was trying to fix something with a wrench,

01:01:06.359 --> 01:01:10.230
and you're pulling with your bicep and your
arm towards you and it broke loose and chipped

01:01:10.230 --> 01:01:11.230
my tooth.

01:01:11.230 --> 01:01:12.230
Keep your head out of the way.

01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:13.230
Tim Ferriss: Good thing you train your neck.

01:01:13.230 --> 01:01:15.750
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, this is mostly a falsey,
this one front tooth.

01:01:15.750 --> 01:01:16.750
Exactly.

01:01:16.750 --> 01:01:17.750
Good thing.

01:01:17.750 --> 01:01:18.750
I could have knocked myself out.

01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:22.480
But there's a lot of things that a bicep is
good for, forearm strength is good for, so

01:01:22.480 --> 01:01:23.599
Joe's absolutely right.

01:01:23.599 --> 01:01:27.619
But I think that when you look at all of that,
it's not that much time in the gym, you can

01:01:27.619 --> 01:01:29.119
do all of that at home.

01:01:29.119 --> 01:01:33.200
There are ways to do the leg workout even
at home or in natural terrain for the hikes

01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:34.200
and things.

01:01:34.200 --> 01:01:36.560
And so that's the schedule.

01:01:36.560 --> 01:01:41.369
And again, if you think about that schedule,
each day you're accomplishing something, endurance,

01:01:41.369 --> 01:01:45.680
leg strength, cold and heat adaptation, and
all the neural stuff that goes with that,

01:01:45.680 --> 01:01:48.180
torso, to keep the torso strong.

01:01:48.180 --> 01:01:53.289
But here again, and we can say the torso work
is indirectly hitting biceps and triceps.

01:01:53.289 --> 01:01:57.329
Then being able to run a couple miles is a
good skill.

01:01:57.329 --> 01:02:00.700
And what you find is that if you trained your
legs properly and you give them enough rest,

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:05.910
and the cold and heat really helped too, you
are so strong on that 20- or 30-minute run,

01:02:05.910 --> 01:02:12.270
your tibs are strong, no floppy feet, no back
pain, you're running with vigor and you go,

01:02:12.270 --> 01:02:13.470
"Wow, this is great."

01:02:13.470 --> 01:02:18.819
And so what I've noticed in the last 24 months
or so is that I continued to get stronger

01:02:18.819 --> 01:02:23.579
and better in each of these areas, and supposedly
that's not supposed to happen.

01:02:23.579 --> 01:02:24.579
I'm 47.

01:02:24.579 --> 01:02:28.599
Attia the other day said, "You and I were
both far more physically robust when we were

01:02:28.599 --> 01:02:29.599
in our teens."

01:02:29.599 --> 01:02:30.599
I was like, "Speak for yourself.

01:02:30.599 --> 01:02:31.720
I was getting injured all the time.

01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:33.059
My body hurt.

01:02:33.059 --> 01:02:34.059
I was unhappy."

01:02:34.059 --> 01:02:36.780
Then again, I was skateboarding then, so I
was slamming a lot.

01:02:36.780 --> 01:02:43.180
But I find that this routine has really helped
me because I think it takes into account the

01:02:43.180 --> 01:02:45.670
slower recovery, that is just me.

01:02:45.670 --> 01:02:48.160
My nervous system doesn't recover that quickly.

01:02:48.160 --> 01:02:54.480
And it also embraces the fact that our nervous
system and musculoskeletal system can train

01:02:54.480 --> 01:02:58.930
for different adaptations simultaneously across
the week.

01:02:58.930 --> 01:03:03.700
Now the one cap on everything that's vital,
and again here a hat tip to Charles Poliquin,

01:03:03.700 --> 01:03:09.091
is if you start training for longer than 60
minutes with resistance training, cortisol

01:03:09.091 --> 01:03:13.270
levels go up, you start feeling lousy, you're
not sleeping well, or you find you can't sleep

01:03:13.270 --> 01:03:15.349
enough and you don't recover.

01:03:15.349 --> 01:03:18.500
So keeping the workouts relatively short has
really helped.

01:03:18.500 --> 01:03:24.270
And then the final thing is that I'm a scientist
slash, I guess now, podcaster and I'm not

01:03:24.270 --> 01:03:28.340
interested in being a competitive athlete,
so I don't want to spend all my time training.

01:03:28.340 --> 01:03:33.359
I really want to be able to get the most out
of my training routine to feel much better

01:03:33.359 --> 01:03:36.450
than I would otherwise when I'm doing cognitive
work.

01:03:36.450 --> 01:03:42.619
And I'm a big believer that this workout schedule
captures most, if not all of the neural circuits,

01:03:42.619 --> 01:03:45.180
that are wanted or one would want to activate.

01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:48.270
Perhaps the one thing missing from it is there
isn't anything in there that's really about

01:03:48.270 --> 01:03:54.329
dynamic movement in multiple planes, I'm not
doing jiu-jitsu, I sometimes play badminton

01:03:54.329 --> 01:03:56.520
or something, badminton is fun.

01:03:56.520 --> 01:04:02.310
I'll do an hour of play type stuff, non-competitive,
low stake stuff, big fan of the book Play

01:04:02.310 --> 01:04:03.310
It Away.

01:04:03.310 --> 01:04:04.460
I think I learned about that book from your
podcast.

01:04:04.460 --> 01:04:05.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:04:05.460 --> 01:04:07.319
Andrew Huberman: Charlie —
Tim Ferriss: Charlie Hoehn.

01:04:07.319 --> 01:04:10.160
Andrew Huberman: Having an hour a week where
you're not trying to get fitter, you're not

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:14.980
trying to burn calories or accomplish any
adaptation, you're just trying to play and

01:04:14.980 --> 01:04:21.050
enjoy your ability to engage in low stakes,
maybe competitive, but generally low stakes

01:04:21.050 --> 01:04:25.619
type movement where you're very focused, which
is how I define play.

01:04:25.619 --> 01:04:29.089
That's also great, but that's not really exercise.

01:04:29.089 --> 01:04:30.150
That's play.

01:04:30.150 --> 01:04:35.380
Tim Ferriss: There's a difference between,
sometimes, recreation and exercise if we're

01:04:35.380 --> 01:04:37.020
talking about provoking adaptations.

01:04:37.020 --> 01:04:38.900
So I just want to underscore a few things.

01:04:38.900 --> 01:04:44.330
One is you talked about nervous system or
central nervous system, CNS recovery, which

01:04:44.330 --> 01:04:51.500
is I just want to make note of that because
I think a lot of folks think in terms of muscular

01:04:51.500 --> 01:04:54.920
recovery but not nervous system recovery.

01:04:54.920 --> 01:04:59.619
And depending on what you're doing, certainly
that's very important.

01:04:59.619 --> 01:05:04.180
Question on a few things that I just took
notes on.

01:05:04.180 --> 01:05:08.880
So Ben Patrick, Knees Over Toes, I might need
to connect with him at some point.

01:05:08.880 --> 01:05:09.910
Andrew Huberman: Oh, yeah, he's terrific.

01:05:09.910 --> 01:05:14.480
Tim Ferriss: I have questions about tib work,
but before I get to that, maybe you can answer

01:05:14.480 --> 01:05:15.549
the question that I have.

01:05:15.549 --> 01:05:18.490
You mentioned Nordic curls in passing.

01:05:18.490 --> 01:05:19.869
What the hell are Nordic curls?

01:05:19.869 --> 01:05:20.869
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:05:20.869 --> 01:05:24.329
Nordics are a great semi-substitute for the
glute-ham raise.

01:05:24.329 --> 01:05:29.530
The glute-ham raise allows you to fix your
feet and then put your head down towards the

01:05:29.530 --> 01:05:34.950
floor and go up into a back raise and then
into what's effectively a hamstring curl.

01:05:34.950 --> 01:05:38.100
It hits the whole posterior chain and the
lower back.

01:05:38.100 --> 01:05:43.059
And Nordic curls are when you either brace
your heels underneath a heavy piece of equipment,

01:05:43.059 --> 01:05:48.470
or you use a lifting belt and strap yourself
to a bench or someone strong enough or heavy

01:05:48.470 --> 01:05:50.380
enough holds your ankles down.

01:05:50.380 --> 01:05:55.180
And you're lowering down and you're touching
your hands to the floor and coming back up.

01:05:55.180 --> 01:05:57.851
Or you can do the Ben Patrick, Ben Bruno.

01:05:57.851 --> 01:06:02.779
Ben Bruno's another excellent Instagram trainer
and professional trainer.

01:06:02.779 --> 01:06:07.200
He trains Justin Timberlake and a bunch of
other kind of famous — not kind of famous

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:08.200
people.

01:06:08.200 --> 01:06:09.200
Justin Timberlake's not kind of famous.

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:10.370
He's famous.

01:06:10.370 --> 01:06:13.450
And he can do the cookie challenge, which
is to put a cookie in your mouth and dip it

01:06:13.450 --> 01:06:15.309
in milk without putting your hands to the
floor.

01:06:15.309 --> 01:06:16.309
Tim Ferriss: That's incredibly hard.

01:06:16.309 --> 01:06:17.309
Andrew Huberman: It's interesting.

01:06:17.309 --> 01:06:25.360
Ben is deceptively strong because he's not
that large, but he's very, very strong.

01:06:25.360 --> 01:06:27.190
BenBrunoTraining is his handle.

01:06:27.190 --> 01:06:28.700
Tim Ferriss: Cue break dancers.

01:06:28.700 --> 01:06:31.870
You want to see some strong people who are
deceptive.

01:06:31.870 --> 01:06:32.870
B-Boys, B-Girls.

01:06:32.870 --> 01:06:34.380
Andrew Huberman: So Nordic curls are great.

01:06:34.380 --> 01:06:39.599
There are now machines that — or an apparatus
you can buy where you can fix your heels under

01:06:39.599 --> 01:06:41.020
something, do Nordic curls.

01:06:41.020 --> 01:06:44.180
Even a portable one for when you travel that
goes under the door.

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:48.520
Ben Patrick, AKA Knees Over Toes Guy, he has
a system called ATG training.

01:06:48.520 --> 01:06:53.230
I confess, I didn't subscribe to ATG because
I've got what I need now.

01:06:53.230 --> 01:07:01.000
But a bunch of those portable equipment type
things are available through him.

01:07:01.000 --> 01:07:05.990
And I think he's really changed the way that
I think about how the whole body moves and

01:07:05.990 --> 01:07:06.990
works.

01:07:06.990 --> 01:07:09.130
He insists that he can get me dunking a basketball,
which, who knows?

01:07:09.130 --> 01:07:11.990
Maybe that should be the challenge before
our next conversation.

01:07:11.990 --> 01:07:17.700
Tim Ferriss: So Nordic curls, unbeknownst
to me, because there's some other term for

01:07:17.700 --> 01:07:22.940
this particular exercise, but I have a piece
of Sorinex equipment that actually sits my

01:07:22.940 --> 01:07:24.890
garage and is for this exact exercise.

01:07:24.890 --> 01:07:29.079
Andrew Huberman: I think if I had to pick
just three — I will never just pick three.

01:07:29.079 --> 01:07:34.490
But if I had to pick three exercises to do
and I could only do those, it would be glute-ham

01:07:34.490 --> 01:07:42.080
raise/Nordic curl, something for the posterior
chain, it would be ring dips because then

01:07:42.080 --> 01:07:44.150
I could throw in some leg raises too, or something
like that.

01:07:44.150 --> 01:07:49.670
I could make it a multi-compound movement
and I would sprint.

01:07:49.670 --> 01:07:53.480
If that's the only thing I could do, that's
what I would do.

01:07:53.480 --> 01:07:57.059
Put me in a small prison cell, knock on wood,
let's hope that doesn't happen.

01:07:57.059 --> 01:07:58.859
That's what I ask for.

01:07:58.859 --> 01:08:01.039
Tim Ferriss: Scientist/podcaster/inmate.

01:08:01.039 --> 01:08:03.859
Andrew Huberman: Hopefully not.

01:08:03.859 --> 01:08:07.150
Listen, we talked last time, I've been behind
locked doors before.

01:08:07.150 --> 01:08:08.960
It's not an experience I want to recreate.

01:08:08.960 --> 01:08:09.960
Tim Ferriss: Not fun.

01:08:09.960 --> 01:08:10.960
Andrew Huberman: Not fun.

01:08:10.960 --> 01:08:11.960
Tim Ferriss: Not an indoorist.

01:08:11.960 --> 01:08:12.960
Andrew Huberman: No.

01:08:12.960 --> 01:08:14.460
Tim Ferriss: So on the tib work side, this
may be a question for Ben or someone more

01:08:14.460 --> 01:08:22.299
qualified, but one of the challenges that
I've had for decades: shin splints.

01:08:22.299 --> 01:08:28.509
And my dorsiflexion is actually seemingly,
from a strength perspective, pretty decent.

01:08:28.509 --> 01:08:30.949
Ankle mobility, also pretty decent.

01:08:30.949 --> 01:08:34.810
Although the left ankle's been broken so many
times that it's a little crunchy.

01:08:34.810 --> 01:08:39.739
But I have done training with this fellow
named Jerzy Gregorek, who holds multiple world

01:08:39.739 --> 01:08:41.429
records in Olympic weightlifting, or he held.

01:08:41.429 --> 01:08:42.940
Andrew Huberman: Also deceptively strong.

01:08:42.940 --> 01:08:44.020
Tim Ferriss: Incredibly strong.

01:08:44.020 --> 01:08:45.020
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:08:45.020 --> 01:08:46.020
I met him and his wife.

01:08:46.020 --> 01:08:49.299
Tim Ferriss: She also has world records in
Olympic weightlifting.

01:08:49.299 --> 01:08:53.400
And just to give you an idea, folks, he's
got to be in his, I want to say, he's probably

01:08:53.400 --> 01:09:00.240
67, 68 now, and he can do a full barbell snatch,
ass-to-heels while on top of an INDO BOARD,

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:02.509
which are those balance boards on top of a
cylinder.

01:09:02.509 --> 01:09:04.040
Andrew Huberman: Sounds very dangerous.

01:09:04.040 --> 01:09:06.370
Tim Ferriss: And I don't recommend trying
to replicate that.

01:09:06.370 --> 01:09:09.630
But he's quite a physical specimen.

01:09:09.630 --> 01:09:14.620
So the point of all of that is that my ankle
mobility's pretty good.

01:09:14.620 --> 01:09:19.569
My dorsiflexion strength seems pretty good,
but I always feel like the front of my shins

01:09:19.569 --> 01:09:21.330
are about to explode.

01:09:21.330 --> 01:09:23.980
Someone could just pop them like a balloon
with a pin.

01:09:23.980 --> 01:09:27.290
And I've not figured out a way to address
this.

01:09:27.290 --> 01:09:28.290
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:09:28.290 --> 01:09:29.290
I vote tib work.

01:09:29.290 --> 01:09:30.290
I used to have terrible shin splints.

01:09:30.290 --> 01:09:31.290
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:32.290
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:09:32.290 --> 01:09:34.620
Terrible shin splints, and also pain in my
shins just from skateboarding, taking so many

01:09:34.620 --> 01:09:39.600
shiners as they're called, from doing a little
bit of Thai boxing when I was in high school

01:09:39.600 --> 01:09:46.029
and college and thinking I was being smart
by conditioning my shins with coke bottles

01:09:46.029 --> 01:09:47.450
like I heard they did it over in Thailand.

01:09:47.450 --> 01:09:48.690
Just made a mess of my shins.

01:09:48.690 --> 01:09:51.540
The tib raises have changed everything.

01:09:51.540 --> 01:09:55.630
Also, I used to think I had flat feet, "I
have flat feet.

01:09:55.630 --> 01:09:57.010
That's the source of my problem."

01:09:57.010 --> 01:09:59.510
And I'd do all this toe strengthening, foot
strengthening stuff.

01:09:59.510 --> 01:10:02.120
All these people in the yoga community in
San Francisco were like, "Oh, I'm going to

01:10:02.120 --> 01:10:03.120
train your feet."

01:10:03.120 --> 01:10:04.680
Turned out, I don't have flat feet.

01:10:04.680 --> 01:10:11.140
It turns out that my tibs were weak and so
the foot wasn't resting in the right position.

01:10:11.140 --> 01:10:14.199
So for me, it's been a game changer.

01:10:14.199 --> 01:10:16.570
Also, you don't need any specialized equipment.

01:10:16.570 --> 01:10:20.010
There is that movement of resting your shoulders
against a wall.

01:10:20.010 --> 01:10:24.400
So you're in a plank, you make your body rigid,
your heels are on the ground about a foot

01:10:24.400 --> 01:10:28.320
out in front of you, and then you're touching
your toes down to the floor and then back

01:10:28.320 --> 01:10:29.320
up.

01:10:29.320 --> 01:10:30.360
And you're just doing that while you're on
the phone.

01:10:30.360 --> 01:10:33.970
You do 25 or 30 repetitions of that, your
tibs will be screaming.

01:10:33.970 --> 01:10:36.710
Tim Ferriss: So it's as if you're basically
a statue.

01:10:36.710 --> 01:10:40.140
You can't articulate any of your joints minus
your ankles.

01:10:40.140 --> 01:10:44.460
And you're just standing, what, like a foot
and a half, two feet away and then kind of

01:10:44.460 --> 01:10:45.780
—
Andrew Huberman: Shoulders against the wall.

01:10:45.780 --> 01:10:48.620
Tim Ferriss: Getting your shoulders to the
wall, but you're leaning like a book against

01:10:48.620 --> 01:10:50.449
a bookend.

01:10:50.449 --> 01:10:51.750
And then moving your toes.

01:10:51.750 --> 01:10:52.750
Andrew Huberman: Yup.

01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:53.750
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:10:53.750 --> 01:10:54.750
I'm in.

01:10:54.750 --> 01:10:55.750
Count me in.

01:10:55.750 --> 01:10:59.590
I am also, in the, apparently, I think this
is possible, the lying to myself about the

01:10:59.590 --> 01:11:00.590
flat feet part.

01:11:00.590 --> 01:11:01.590
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:11:01.590 --> 01:11:02.679
I was convinced I have flat feet.

01:11:02.679 --> 01:11:05.320
You want to hear something really wild that
has nothing to do with fitness, but has everything

01:11:05.320 --> 01:11:07.390
to do with metabolism and the obesity crisis?

01:11:07.390 --> 01:11:09.949
Tim Ferriss: Wild metabolism and obesity crisis,
tell me.

01:11:09.949 --> 01:11:10.949
Andrew Huberman: And tibs.

01:11:10.949 --> 01:11:15.949
There's a very important paper published from
the University of Houston this last year where

01:11:15.949 --> 01:11:22.679
they had people sitting for a couple of hours
and every two or three seconds they would

01:11:22.679 --> 01:11:25.870
keep their toe on the ground and they would
do what was effectively a seated calf raise.

01:11:25.870 --> 01:11:29.800
Think about the jumpy kid in class or when
you've had too much coffee.

01:11:29.800 --> 01:11:35.320
They're doing it slowly and they're measuring
the contraction of the soleus, the longer,

01:11:35.320 --> 01:11:38.409
flatter muscle of the calf underneath the
gastro.

01:11:38.409 --> 01:11:40.350
Turns out that muscle is very unique.

01:11:40.350 --> 01:11:45.429
It does not use the same fuel sources as other
muscles in the same way.

01:11:45.429 --> 01:11:48.449
It's not so much a glycogen-dependent muscle.

01:11:48.449 --> 01:11:54.290
It is designed, of course, to carry you very
long distances over — it's an endurance

01:11:54.290 --> 01:11:55.290
muscle.

01:11:55.290 --> 01:12:00.030
They had people do this while seated for a
couple hours a day, and they looked at glucose

01:12:00.030 --> 01:12:06.920
uptake and they looked at overall insulin
management, and there was a significant and

01:12:06.920 --> 01:12:11.320
meaningful improvement in insulin sensitivity.

01:12:11.320 --> 01:12:14.100
Now, this is not about caloric burn.

01:12:14.100 --> 01:12:17.820
This is about essentially doing exercise while
seated.

01:12:17.820 --> 01:12:22.239
Now I put out some stuff about this on social
media and people understandably laugh, like,

01:12:22.239 --> 01:12:23.239
"Oh, that's ridiculous."

01:12:23.239 --> 01:12:26.080
First of all, they call it a soleus pushup
in the study.

01:12:26.080 --> 01:12:27.670
You're like, "That's a seated calf raise."

01:12:27.670 --> 01:12:29.330
But most people don't know what a seated calf
raise is.

01:12:29.330 --> 01:12:32.860
So gym rats, you're only laughing to yourselves.

01:12:32.860 --> 01:12:34.670
I throw myself in that.

01:12:34.670 --> 01:12:35.670
I mean, it's true.

01:12:35.670 --> 01:12:36.870
Tim Ferriss: Most of the internet.

01:12:36.870 --> 01:12:37.949
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, exactly.

01:12:37.949 --> 01:12:38.949
Exactly.

01:12:38.949 --> 01:12:44.260
But what's very interesting is this is something
that a lot of people can do who are trying

01:12:44.260 --> 01:12:47.140
to improve their metabolic status.

01:12:47.140 --> 01:12:52.220
Of course, they should also exercise, but
as the paper describes, it's no surprise why

01:12:52.220 --> 01:12:53.530
this works.

01:12:53.530 --> 01:12:57.050
Were we quote/unquote "designed" to walk around
more or move more during the day?

01:12:57.050 --> 01:12:58.050
I don't know.

01:12:58.050 --> 01:13:03.550
I wasn't consulted at the design phase, but
we were definitely moving around a lot more

01:13:03.550 --> 01:13:05.440
than we probably are now.

01:13:05.440 --> 01:13:07.320
But it's very interesting that — this muscle's
small.

01:13:07.320 --> 01:13:13.480
It's only one percent of the body's total
musculature, can account for well over 15

01:13:13.480 --> 01:13:17.070
percent — well, over, it depends on the
person, but at least 15 percent of your total

01:13:17.070 --> 01:13:18.660
energy expenditure during the day.

01:13:18.660 --> 01:13:19.660
Tim Ferriss: That's insane.

01:13:19.660 --> 01:13:21.720
Andrew Huberman: So if you're on a plane like
this and you're bouncing your knee or doing

01:13:21.720 --> 01:13:26.250
this thing, it's actually a meaningful — it's
not a replacement for exercise, but in terms

01:13:26.250 --> 01:13:29.699
of its metabolic impact, is meaningful.

01:13:29.699 --> 01:13:35.230
I find that really interesting and perhaps
of all the things that we'll discuss today,

01:13:35.230 --> 01:13:39.980
if you're unwilling to try that, then your
bar for entry is just way too high.

01:13:39.980 --> 01:13:43.070
Tim Ferriss: So have a few extra cups of coffee,
folks.

01:13:43.070 --> 01:13:44.070
Bounce those knees.

01:13:44.070 --> 01:13:45.070
Andrew Huberman: Exactly.

01:13:45.070 --> 01:13:47.370
And don't get on kids about bouncing their
knees in class, teachers.

01:13:47.370 --> 01:13:48.840
I was one such a kid.

01:13:48.840 --> 01:13:56.770
Tim Ferriss: All right, so we've covered a
bunch of exercise and a number of things as

01:13:56.770 --> 01:14:01.420
fundamental pillars that you've doubled down
on.

01:14:01.420 --> 01:14:03.040
Anything that you've changed your mind on?

01:14:03.040 --> 01:14:08.730
Andrew Huberman: Well, one of them was the
fact that I always believed that I had flat

01:14:08.730 --> 01:14:09.730
feet.

01:14:09.730 --> 01:14:13.280
I also believed that we couldn't obtain multiple
exercise adaptations at once.

01:14:13.280 --> 01:14:15.659
I'm convinced we can because I track my numbers.

01:14:15.659 --> 01:14:19.010
I'm not super neurotic, but I keep a handwritten
training journal.

01:14:19.010 --> 01:14:23.090
Or at the end of the day, I generally can
remember what I did in the gym and didn't

01:14:23.090 --> 01:14:24.370
do, or on a run.

01:14:24.370 --> 01:14:26.430
And I'm improving over time.

01:14:26.430 --> 01:14:27.430
So that's great.

01:14:27.430 --> 01:14:33.790
I thought I would have had hit my peak ability
and it was all downhill from here, but I feel

01:14:33.790 --> 01:14:34.790
much better.

01:14:34.790 --> 01:14:37.160
Tim Ferriss: How do you track, if you track
the Tabata, for instance?

01:14:37.160 --> 01:14:38.160
Are you looking at wattage?

01:14:38.160 --> 01:14:39.160
Are you looking at anything?

01:14:39.160 --> 01:14:40.160
Or is it more subjective?

01:14:40.160 --> 01:14:42.510
Andrew Huberman: I tend to add another round
or two.

01:14:42.510 --> 01:14:44.930
I just do a little bit more work or go a little
bit harder.

01:14:44.930 --> 01:14:49.150
I tend like to do things pretty subjectively
when it comes to exercise.

01:14:49.150 --> 01:14:55.090
My calendar will say, for instance, for legs,
it'll say tib, calf, ham, quad.

01:14:55.090 --> 01:14:57.960
And I'll put anywhere from level one to level
10.

01:14:57.960 --> 01:15:01.600
Level 10 would be the workout with forced
reps and drop sets, just all out.

01:15:01.600 --> 01:15:03.050
I rarely, if ever, do those.

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:07.850
But typically I'm trying to make that workout
at least a level 7.5 to nine.

01:15:07.850 --> 01:15:10.820
And generally, the shorter workouts are the
harder ones.

01:15:10.820 --> 01:15:15.120
Occasionally, just because of schedules or
I'm not feeling great, I'll do that Wednesday

01:15:15.120 --> 01:15:21.110
run and it'll end up being a 15-minute jog
or something, and I'll just say, "Level five.

01:15:21.110 --> 01:15:24.310
Lame," or something like that, like I didn't
— or something like that.

01:15:24.310 --> 01:15:26.400
And then push a little bit harder the next
day.

01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:28.330
So I'm kind of my own coach.

01:15:28.330 --> 01:15:31.760
I'm sort of observing myself rather than getting
really deep into the metrics.

01:15:31.760 --> 01:15:35.750
But I'm always trying to put a little bit
more weight on the bar, go a little bit slower

01:15:35.750 --> 01:15:41.810
with the repetition, or add a set or a rep
or two, or all three.

01:15:41.810 --> 01:15:49.710
Tim Ferriss: So let's hone in on sleep, my
perennial favorite and the bane of my well-being,

01:15:49.710 --> 01:15:51.330
depending on the quality.

01:15:51.330 --> 01:15:58.020
But last we spoke, I remember you spoke about,
I think it was magnesium threonate, apigenin,

01:15:58.020 --> 01:15:59.739
and there may have been one or two or two
other —

01:15:59.739 --> 01:16:00.739
Andrew Huberman: Theanine.

01:16:00.739 --> 01:16:01.739
Tim Ferriss: — Lucky Charms in there.

01:16:01.739 --> 01:16:02.739
Andrew Huberman: Theanine.

01:16:02.739 --> 01:16:03.739
Tim Ferriss: Right, theanine.

01:16:03.739 --> 01:16:04.739
Andrew Huberman: Yep.

01:16:04.739 --> 01:16:07.920
Tim Ferriss: Are those still the holy trinity
for your sleep, or do you have other things

01:16:07.920 --> 01:16:10.320
that you've added, things you've subtracted?

01:16:10.320 --> 01:16:11.810
What is your current cocktail?

01:16:11.810 --> 01:16:18.380
With the understanding that as much as people
want to fix everything with silver bullets,

01:16:18.380 --> 01:16:24.250
light exposure in the morning, exercise, all
of these are contributors to good quality

01:16:24.250 --> 01:16:25.250
sleep.

01:16:25.250 --> 01:16:27.670
Andrew Huberman: I still use the same sleep
stack.

01:16:27.670 --> 01:16:35.100
So it's magnesium threonate, spelled T-H-R-E-O-N-A-T-E,
theanine, and apigenin.

01:16:35.100 --> 01:16:41.889
And I've added every once in a while, I'll
take 900 milligrams of myo-inositol.

01:16:41.889 --> 01:16:44.179
Myo-inositol has a rich literature associated
—

01:16:44.179 --> 01:16:45.179
Tim Ferriss: Interesting name.

01:16:45.179 --> 01:16:46.179
Myo as in muscle?

01:16:46.179 --> 01:16:47.179
Why myo?

01:16:47.179 --> 01:16:48.179
M-Y-O.

01:16:48.179 --> 01:16:49.179
Andrew Huberman: That's a good question.

01:16:49.179 --> 01:16:50.179
Yeah, it must be.

01:16:50.179 --> 01:16:51.179
Tim Ferriss: It must be.

01:16:51.179 --> 01:16:56.120
Andrew Huberman: So it's amino acid, but it
can be a neurotransmitter mimetic, extensive

01:16:56.120 --> 01:16:59.739
literature on inositol for improving insulin
sensitivity.

01:16:59.739 --> 01:17:04.930
There's also something called D-chiro inositol,
which is important for female fertility.

01:17:04.930 --> 01:17:06.190
We can talk about that.

01:17:06.190 --> 01:17:11.660
But what I've added in the 900 milligrams
of myo-inositol for is I do sometimes, like

01:17:11.660 --> 01:17:16.220
many people, wake up at three in the morning
to use the restroom.

01:17:16.220 --> 01:17:19.270
And although there's a simple solution to
that that I just recently learned that really

01:17:19.270 --> 01:17:22.389
works, and I'll share it in a moment —
Tim Ferriss: Catheter.

01:17:22.389 --> 01:17:24.989
Andrew Huberman: — it helps me fall back
asleep after that.

01:17:24.989 --> 01:17:26.460
And I am tracking my sleep.

01:17:26.460 --> 01:17:28.659
Because I sleep on an Eight Sleep.

01:17:28.659 --> 01:17:31.550
And that has good sleep tracking ability.

01:17:31.550 --> 01:17:36.790
What Attia has taught me, that a lot of the
wristbands and rings for tracking sleep, while

01:17:36.790 --> 01:17:40.870
they can be quite good and are quite good,
WHOOPs and Ouras being the two most popular

01:17:40.870 --> 01:17:45.490
ones, they can get movement wrong because
of movement of limbs.

01:17:45.490 --> 01:17:50.400
Whereas the Eight Sleep seems to really capture
slow wave sleep, rapid eye movement, sleep

01:17:50.400 --> 01:17:51.610
ratios really well.

01:17:51.610 --> 01:17:54.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I use both Eight Sleep
and Oura at the moment.

01:17:54.760 --> 01:17:56.100
Andrew Huberman: Great.

01:17:56.100 --> 01:17:57.100
Yeah.

01:17:57.100 --> 01:18:01.740
So the myo-inositol, it has helped quite a
bit with that going back to sleep thing, one

01:18:01.740 --> 01:18:06.030
way to not wake up to urinate so much in the
middle of the night I learned from a colleague

01:18:06.030 --> 01:18:10.710
is it turns out that it's not just how much
fluid you drink, which dictates whether or

01:18:10.710 --> 01:18:13.630
not you need to urinate because that's sort
of a duh.

01:18:13.630 --> 01:18:19.140
It's also how quickly you ingest that fluid
if you gulp fluid down because of the way

01:18:19.140 --> 01:18:23.699
fluid is absorbed, it actually, in the kidney,
and the way filtration occurs in the kidney,

01:18:23.699 --> 01:18:27.750
the way it signals to the bladder is that
it makes you have to go to the bathroom fairly

01:18:27.750 --> 01:18:28.750
urgently.

01:18:28.750 --> 01:18:33.750
So if you have your final beverage of the
day, sip it, don't gulp it.

01:18:33.750 --> 01:18:35.450
For morning hydration, the opposite is true.

01:18:35.450 --> 01:18:37.610
You actually want to gulp down quite a lot
of liquid in the morning.

01:18:37.610 --> 01:18:39.900
You can absorb quite a lot of liquid in the
morning.

01:18:39.900 --> 01:18:46.350
I'm reading this really nerdy review in Nature,
it has these wonderful review series on circadian

01:18:46.350 --> 01:18:47.719
rhythms and the kidney.

01:18:47.719 --> 01:18:51.080
Your kidney is not the same organ first thing
in the morning as it is at night.

01:18:51.080 --> 01:18:55.260
So this is what circadian biologists have
been shouting for a long time, every organ

01:18:55.260 --> 01:18:56.280
is this way.

01:18:56.280 --> 01:18:59.780
But you want to hydrate pretty heavily first
thing in the morning, and then over the day

01:18:59.780 --> 01:19:01.870
you can titrate off that liquid.

01:19:01.870 --> 01:19:05.040
Tim Ferriss: So I'm going to ask you to pick
some favorite children here.

01:19:05.040 --> 01:19:13.620
So you've got the magnesium threonate, L-theanine,
apigenin, which am I understanding correctly,

01:19:13.620 --> 01:19:17.260
that is effectively chamomile tea on steroids?

01:19:17.260 --> 01:19:18.260
Andrew Huberman: Mm-hmm.

01:19:18.260 --> 01:19:19.350
High concentration chamomile extract.

01:19:19.350 --> 01:19:20.520
Tim Ferriss: All right, got it.

01:19:20.520 --> 01:19:24.360
And then the myo-inositol —
Andrew Huberman: Myo-inositol.

01:19:24.360 --> 01:19:25.440
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:19:25.440 --> 01:19:28.480
Andrew Huberman: 900 milligrams, which it's
a big pill, but turns out to be pretty low

01:19:28.480 --> 01:19:34.750
dose for insulin sensitivity, and for depression,
they give people up to five grams of myo-inositol

01:19:34.750 --> 01:19:35.750
a day.

01:19:35.750 --> 01:19:37.180
But it's a mild sedative.

01:19:37.180 --> 01:19:39.159
So I don't know how people get away with that.

01:19:39.159 --> 01:19:40.780
I'll take the four of those.

01:19:40.780 --> 01:19:44.030
I will occasionally be so fired —
Tim Ferriss: Pop five Trazodone before my

01:19:44.030 --> 01:19:45.030
workout, yeah.

01:19:45.030 --> 01:19:47.120
Andrew Huberman: Occasionally, I'm so tired
that I'll wake up with the pills in my hand

01:19:47.120 --> 01:19:48.469
or on the nightstand.

01:19:48.469 --> 01:19:51.969
So I don't think I'm dependent on them in
any way.

01:19:51.969 --> 01:20:02.360
Tim Ferriss: The question is: if you could
only choose two of those?

01:20:02.360 --> 01:20:03.969
Andrew Huberman: Oh, easy.

01:20:03.969 --> 01:20:05.030
Mag threonate and apigenin.

01:20:05.030 --> 01:20:06.030
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:20:06.030 --> 01:20:07.030
Yeah.

01:20:07.030 --> 01:20:08.030
Got it.

01:20:08.030 --> 01:20:10.320
Andrew Huberman: Theanine is not going to
be good for people that have very robust dreams

01:20:10.320 --> 01:20:15.960
or nightmares because it will increase how
vivid your dreams are.

01:20:15.960 --> 01:20:19.180
So night walkers, night terrors, that kind
of thing.

01:20:19.180 --> 01:20:22.190
For those of you that have sleepwalking, night
walkers —

01:20:22.190 --> 01:20:23.520
Tim Ferriss: Skinwalkers, beware.

01:20:23.520 --> 01:20:24.520
Andrew Huberman: Sorry.

01:20:24.520 --> 01:20:26.580
And I didn't mean to interrupt you.

01:20:26.580 --> 01:20:31.310
This is something I'm working, my colleague
at Stanford, Carol Dweck, told me that it's

01:20:31.310 --> 01:20:33.000
a sign of enthusiasm for the conversation.

01:20:33.000 --> 01:20:34.000
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, no.

01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:35.020
Andrew Huberman: So I apologize.

01:20:35.020 --> 01:20:36.580
Tim Ferriss: Oh, are you kidding?

01:20:36.580 --> 01:20:39.140
I'm not going to throw stones in my glass
house here.

01:20:39.140 --> 01:20:40.230
I interrupt constantly.

01:20:40.230 --> 01:20:46.510
But you were talking about the kidneys, you
were saying not the same organ in the morning

01:20:46.510 --> 01:20:47.510
as it is at night.

01:20:47.510 --> 01:20:53.120
This is what circadian biologists have been
screaming from the rooftops, and now that's

01:20:53.120 --> 01:20:54.120
coming home to roost.

01:20:54.120 --> 01:20:59.180
And these papers that you're reading, or maybe
it's a meta-analysis, I don't know.

01:20:59.180 --> 01:21:03.480
And you were saying sipping water at night
or just slower intake.

01:21:03.480 --> 01:21:08.560
Gulping in the morning, and I threw your train
of thought off the rails.

01:21:08.560 --> 01:21:09.560
Andrew Huberman: All good.

01:21:09.560 --> 01:21:11.850
I mean, basically you want to look at the
first half of your day very differently than

01:21:11.850 --> 01:21:13.850
the second half of your day.

01:21:13.850 --> 01:21:19.100
Morning part of your day, you want sunlight
or bright artificial light.

01:21:19.100 --> 01:21:20.100
Why?

01:21:20.100 --> 01:21:22.840
It increases cortisol 50 percent above baseline.

01:21:22.840 --> 01:21:27.429
You want cortisol high early in the day and
you want it low, low later in the day.

01:21:27.429 --> 01:21:32.150
Not because it disrupts sleep, but because
late-shifted cortisol is associated with depression.

01:21:32.150 --> 01:21:33.780
Screws up your immune system to have it late-shifted.

01:21:33.780 --> 01:21:38.050
Beautiful work from Bob Sapolsky and David
Spiegel at Stanford School of Medicine have

01:21:38.050 --> 01:21:39.170
shown that.

01:21:39.170 --> 01:21:46.130
So hydration, caffeine, sunlight, movement,
bright light if you can't get sunlight early

01:21:46.130 --> 01:21:47.270
in the day.

01:21:47.270 --> 01:21:51.800
You really want what are called the catecholamines,
which are dopamine, epinephrine, norepinephrine.

01:21:51.800 --> 01:21:57.230
And you want cortisol, which is a glucocorticoid,
elevated in the early part of the day, and

01:21:57.230 --> 01:22:00.210
that's what's going to give you energy, focus,
alertness, all that great stuff throughout

01:22:00.210 --> 01:22:01.210
the day.

01:22:01.210 --> 01:22:04.150
And then you want to taper that stuff off
as the day goes on.

01:22:04.150 --> 01:22:08.919
Now the recent data have shown that if you
want to improve your rapid eye movement sleep

01:22:08.919 --> 01:22:09.929
— why would you want to do that?

01:22:09.929 --> 01:22:15.389
Well, rapid eye movement sleep is when you
get the unpacking of emotions from previous-day

01:22:15.389 --> 01:22:16.389
memories.

01:22:16.389 --> 01:22:19.449
This is your —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, read or listen to our mutual

01:22:19.449 --> 01:22:20.449
friend Matt Walker.

01:22:20.449 --> 01:22:21.449
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:22:21.449 --> 01:22:23.969
How do you get more rapid eye movement sleep?

01:22:23.969 --> 01:22:29.210
High-intensity interval training early part
of the day or cycling appears to greatly improve

01:22:29.210 --> 01:22:31.211
different stages of sleep as well, compared
to running.

01:22:31.211 --> 01:22:32.211
Although running will do it as well.

01:22:32.211 --> 01:22:33.211
Tim Ferriss: Why is that?

01:22:33.211 --> 01:22:34.211
Andrew Huberman: I don't know.

01:22:34.211 --> 01:22:37.160
I think it has to do with the central pattern
generator thing.

01:22:37.160 --> 01:22:40.480
That high-intensity interval training, when
you're peddling on the bike or you're sprinting,

01:22:40.480 --> 01:22:43.531
so it's repetitive, but it's not repetitive
for long enough that you're engaging certain

01:22:43.531 --> 01:22:46.410
brain circuits.

01:22:46.410 --> 01:22:49.219
And it's going to deplete different neurotransmitter
systems.

01:22:49.219 --> 01:22:51.100
It's going to engage the endorphin system
rather than —

01:22:51.100 --> 01:22:56.679
Tim Ferriss: I wonder if it's also a study
bias in the sense that it's so much easier

01:22:56.679 --> 01:23:03.870
to get your slaves, AKA, like, undergrads
or grad students or whoever recruited in a

01:23:03.870 --> 01:23:06.699
university setting to sit on a bike.

01:23:06.699 --> 01:23:11.550
So if you have a hundred studies with bikes
and you have five studies with running that

01:23:11.550 --> 01:23:16.730
maybe it's a selection bias in some respect
because there's just such a higher volume

01:23:16.730 --> 01:23:17.730
of studies.

01:23:17.730 --> 01:23:18.730
Andrew Huberman: I don't know.

01:23:18.730 --> 01:23:19.730
I don't know.

01:23:19.730 --> 01:23:22.449
I love bicycling when I can be upright.

01:23:22.449 --> 01:23:26.489
Think your Dutch bicycle, moving through a
city, seeing things, optic flow, which we

01:23:26.489 --> 01:23:29.929
know shuts down the amygdala to some extent,
suppresses activity in the amygdala.

01:23:29.929 --> 01:23:30.929
It's beautiful.

01:23:30.929 --> 01:23:32.580
You see things, you see people.

01:23:32.580 --> 01:23:37.969
How anyone in the world could want to be hunched
over in a partial seat position and pedaling

01:23:37.969 --> 01:23:39.570
as one is on a road bike.

01:23:39.570 --> 01:23:43.340
I have zero minus one interest in doing that.

01:23:43.340 --> 01:23:44.340
Sounds horrible.

01:23:44.340 --> 01:23:49.120
And then I heard it gives you all these prostate
issues and it can give erectile dysfunction

01:23:49.120 --> 01:23:50.120
from the pain.

01:23:50.120 --> 01:23:54.030
They now put grooves in the seats because
— I just think, why would anyone be a cyclist?

01:23:54.030 --> 01:23:55.030
This is terrible.

01:23:55.030 --> 01:23:58.739
Tim Ferriss: Turns out having a pool cue shoved
into your perennium for hours at a time isn't

01:23:58.739 --> 01:23:59.739
good for you.

01:23:59.739 --> 01:24:01.450
Andrew Huberman: No, hunched over, like a
seat.

01:24:01.450 --> 01:24:04.850
And I realize that I have many friends who
are triathletes and cyclists, so I realize

01:24:04.850 --> 01:24:06.600
there's really an allure there.

01:24:06.600 --> 01:24:11.409
Moving fast through space feels really good,
but also the danger of cars — so riding

01:24:11.409 --> 01:24:16.510
a bicycle recreationally to me makes sense,
as does walking or running or skateboarding

01:24:16.510 --> 01:24:17.510
or whatever.

01:24:17.510 --> 01:24:21.889
But to me, cycling hunched over like that
just seems like a dreadful form of exercise.

01:24:21.889 --> 01:24:23.190
But that's just me.

01:24:23.190 --> 01:24:28.659
Tim Ferriss: So let's go from the dreadful
