At least I'm not ordering chicken teriyaki.

01:27:40.260 --> 01:27:41.260
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:27:41.260 --> 01:27:43.770
And just so people are hearing it from you
and not from me, not to say it's even the

01:27:43.770 --> 01:27:44.770
same thing.

01:27:44.770 --> 01:27:49.300
What are the primary benefits that you observe
or that are supported by literature?

01:27:49.300 --> 01:27:54.130
Either or, with the intake of say, one gram
minimum of EPA per day?

01:27:54.130 --> 01:27:55.320
Andrew Huberman: Mood.

01:27:55.320 --> 01:27:56.320
Antidepressant effects.

01:27:56.320 --> 01:27:57.320
Absolutely clear.

01:27:57.320 --> 01:28:01.290
And there are so many clinical trials supporting
that, people can get away with taking lower

01:28:01.290 --> 01:28:03.870
doses of antidepressants or even coming off
antidepressants.

01:28:03.870 --> 01:28:09.500
Of course, always check with your psychiatrist
before you do that by increasing their omega-3

01:28:09.500 --> 01:28:12.570
intake above one gram per day, even as high
three grams per day.

01:28:12.570 --> 01:28:13.570
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:28:13.570 --> 01:28:14.570
So question for you.

01:28:14.570 --> 01:28:20.690
I remember last time we spoke, we chatted
a bit about the ability for the gut.

01:28:20.690 --> 01:28:29.630
Let's just say, some people refer to it as
the second brain, to sense the intake of sugar,

01:28:29.630 --> 01:28:34.369
fat, and a handful of other things.

01:28:34.369 --> 01:28:40.310
What is the mechanism of action by which the
EPA is affecting mood?

01:28:40.310 --> 01:28:42.520
Is that known or is —
Andrew Huberman: I think so.

01:28:42.520 --> 01:28:44.350
It's probably two-pronged.

01:28:44.350 --> 01:28:52.980
One, the EPA is a fundamental structural lipid
for neuronal membranes and for the neurons

01:28:52.980 --> 01:28:58.489
in particular, that release neuromodulators
such as serotonin and dopamine.

01:28:58.489 --> 01:28:59.489
That's one.

01:28:59.489 --> 01:29:06.369
The other is that these neuropod cells in
the gut, as they're called, since, as you

01:29:06.369 --> 01:29:13.550
pointed out, sugar, essential fatty acids,
and essential amino acids signal via the vagus

01:29:13.550 --> 01:29:15.440
to the dopamine centers of the brain.

01:29:15.440 --> 01:29:19.820
So your gut is subconsciously signaling to
your brain what kinds of nutrients are coming

01:29:19.820 --> 01:29:21.639
into your system.

01:29:21.639 --> 01:29:29.510
And when you have a lot of flavorful food
and/or high caloric food, but it's not high

01:29:29.510 --> 01:29:33.980
in nutrients, it sets that system totally
out of whack because there's an increase in

01:29:33.980 --> 01:29:40.100
dopamine for sure, from the taste of the food
that's not matched by that subconscious parallel

01:29:40.100 --> 01:29:41.100
signal.

01:29:41.100 --> 01:29:44.770
And by the way, this is not woo-woo biology.

01:29:44.770 --> 01:29:50.469
This is coming from the laboratories of Diego
Bohorquez at Duke University School of Medicine.

01:29:50.469 --> 01:29:54.370
Charles Zucker, who's a Howard Hughes medical
investigator at Columbia University, has done

01:29:54.370 --> 01:29:56.219
this for sugar sensing.

01:29:56.219 --> 01:29:59.980
And a number of other laboratories have shown
that this pathway from the subconscious signaling

01:29:59.980 --> 01:30:04.990
from the vagus to the dopamine centers of
the brain are driving an appetite for certain

01:30:04.990 --> 01:30:06.369
kinds of foods.

01:30:06.369 --> 01:30:10.949
However, when these neurons, these neuropod
cells in the gut are "satisfied," they're

01:30:10.949 --> 01:30:16.500
seeing the nutrients they want to see, they
signal to the brain dopamine release within

01:30:16.500 --> 01:30:20.750
the brain, but they also are signaling satiety.

01:30:20.750 --> 01:30:22.210
And that's really what you want.

01:30:22.210 --> 01:30:28.170
So in many ways, we are a essential amino
acid, essential fatty acid foraging machine.

01:30:28.170 --> 01:30:33.640
It just also so happens that at least in the
short term sugar will trigger the same pathway.

01:30:33.640 --> 01:30:40.580
This is also why people who increase their
intake of omega-3s, get above that one gram

01:30:40.580 --> 01:30:47.830
per day of EPAs, or who take small amounts
of L-glutamine, reduce their sugar cravings.

01:30:47.830 --> 01:30:49.280
You're activating the same neurons.

01:30:49.280 --> 01:30:50.520
You're giving the alternative stimulus.

01:30:50.520 --> 01:30:54.050
Tim Ferriss: So the L-glutamine is vis-a-vis
the neuropods.

01:30:54.050 --> 01:30:55.429
Andrew Huberman: Likely.

01:30:55.429 --> 01:30:59.590
That hasn't been directly established, but
it's long been known that increasing intake

01:30:59.590 --> 01:31:04.739
of certain essential amino acids can reduce
craving for sweets.

01:31:04.739 --> 01:31:07.739
And whenever people say, "How do I kill my
sweet craving?"

01:31:07.739 --> 01:31:14.469
Well, it's not a conventional approach, but
you get a quality branch chain amino acid

01:31:14.469 --> 01:31:18.780
or essential amino acid powder, which sometimes
have some non-caloric sweetener or something

01:31:18.780 --> 01:31:20.420
and fruity taste and mix it in there.

01:31:20.420 --> 01:31:25.030
People will find, oh, yeah, I don't actually
crave chocolate the same way.

01:31:25.030 --> 01:31:27.860
In my opinion, a little bit of dark chocolate
is wonderful every once in a while.

01:31:27.860 --> 01:31:30.570
So I don't want to give the impression that
you're never supposed to have sugar.

01:31:30.570 --> 01:31:34.380
But there are people who very much feel a
slave to their sugar cravings.

01:31:34.380 --> 01:31:38.940
So giving these neurons the alternative stimulus
really helps and you're getting effectively

01:31:38.940 --> 01:31:40.880
the same dopamine release.

01:31:40.880 --> 01:31:44.910
This makes sense evolutionarily, why we would
crave essential fatty acids and essential

01:31:44.910 --> 01:31:45.910
amino acids.

01:31:45.910 --> 01:31:46.910
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

01:31:46.910 --> 01:31:50.480
Andrew Huberman: This is also why, the non-meat
eaters won't relate, but a really great steak

01:31:50.480 --> 01:31:55.889
is very satisfying despite the fact that it
has no glucose, essentially.

01:31:55.889 --> 01:31:59.441
There are people who feel quite good, whether
or not they're healthy or not, we could talk

01:31:59.441 --> 01:32:02.889
about, but who feel quite good just eating
steak.

01:32:02.889 --> 01:32:05.800
Now, I'm not one of those people.

01:32:05.800 --> 01:32:10.050
But if you had to pick a food that would keep
you feeling good and would repair the tissues

01:32:10.050 --> 01:32:14.810
of your body and would give you enough fats
to keep going and protein synthesis, et cetera,

01:32:14.810 --> 01:32:17.940
if you had to feed your children, you would
give them a steak.

01:32:17.940 --> 01:32:20.900
You would not give them a sweet potato.

01:32:20.900 --> 01:32:26.400
And if you look at the amount of food that
one eats when given as much meat as they want,

01:32:26.400 --> 01:32:33.110
fatty meat in particular, not gross fatty
meat, but high-quality fatty meat versus carbohydrates,

01:32:33.110 --> 01:32:36.980
you're quite satisfied after a certain amount
of protein intake.

01:32:36.980 --> 01:32:39.260
So I'm not pushing people towards a carnivore
diet.

01:32:39.260 --> 01:32:40.850
I don't follow a carnivore diet.

01:32:40.850 --> 01:32:47.840
But I do think that our nervous system, from
the gut to the brain, is a sensing and foraging

01:32:47.840 --> 01:32:53.679
system that subconsciously and lets our brain
know, "Aha, I've got enough of what I need

01:32:53.679 --> 01:32:55.600
and now I can stop eating if I want to."

01:32:55.600 --> 01:32:59.659
Tim Ferriss: So we're going to leapfrog from
delicious fish.

01:32:59.659 --> 01:33:06.840
I think the move for me might be to go to
just pure liquid refrigerated.

01:33:06.840 --> 01:33:07.840
Try that.

01:33:07.840 --> 01:33:08.950
I mean, who the hell knows.

01:33:08.950 --> 01:33:09.950
The fish oil.

01:33:09.950 --> 01:33:12.430
Just to try something, even if the expiration
date looks fine.

01:33:12.430 --> 01:33:14.540
Maybe I just got a couple of bad batches.

01:33:14.540 --> 01:33:15.540
Who knows?

01:33:15.540 --> 01:33:16.870
Andrew Huberman: Or maybe it's the capsules
themselves.

01:33:16.870 --> 01:33:21.219
There's so many things going into these capsule
formulas and then there's shelf life and all

01:33:21.219 --> 01:33:22.219
of that.

01:33:22.219 --> 01:33:28.719
But gut health overall, it's hard for most
people to do what they need to for their gut.

01:33:28.719 --> 01:33:32.560
In addition to all the things to avoid, too
frequent use of antibiotics and things of

01:33:32.560 --> 01:33:38.100
that source, it's pretty clear that one serving
of fermented foods a day is not going to be

01:33:38.100 --> 01:33:39.100
enough.

01:33:39.100 --> 01:33:42.619
That you need three or four servings of low-sugar
fermented food.

01:33:42.619 --> 01:33:48.120
So nattō for people that can stomach it,
sauerkraut, kimchi.

01:33:48.120 --> 01:33:49.619
A good Bulgarian yogurt.

01:33:49.619 --> 01:33:51.300
I like these really sour Bulgarian yogurts.

01:33:51.300 --> 01:33:52.300
They're so good.

01:33:52.300 --> 01:33:53.300
Tim Ferriss: Bulgarian yogurt?

01:33:53.300 --> 01:33:54.760
Andrew Huberman: Oh, there's this amazing
Bulgarian yogurt.

01:33:54.760 --> 01:33:55.760
You have to try this stuff.

01:33:55.760 --> 01:33:56.760
It is so good.

01:33:56.760 --> 01:33:58.630
Except they don't have — the full-fat one
in particular —

01:33:58.630 --> 01:34:00.739
Tim Ferriss: What makes the Bulgarian yogurt
so magical?

01:34:00.739 --> 01:34:02.270
Andrew Huberman: I wish I could remember the
name of this brand.

01:34:02.270 --> 01:34:03.560
Tim Ferriss: We'll put it in the show notes.

01:34:03.560 --> 01:34:05.300
Andrew Huberman: It's so good.

01:34:05.300 --> 01:34:06.770
But I don't want people to — they run out.

01:34:06.770 --> 01:34:09.159
Because they only put two or three of these
in every Whole Foods.

01:34:09.159 --> 01:34:12.690
And I'm buying those two or three, of the
full-fat ones.

01:34:12.690 --> 01:34:16.760
But it's hard for most people to get that
much low-sugar fermented food per day.

01:34:16.760 --> 01:34:18.619
And so most people just don't do it.

01:34:18.619 --> 01:34:23.820
And then high-dose probiotics are very expensive,
need to be refrigerated.

01:34:23.820 --> 01:34:27.490
So that's why I think the AG1 checks off a
number of boxes there.

01:34:27.490 --> 01:34:31.460
But if people can ingest low-sugar fermented
foods, that's going to serve them really,

01:34:31.460 --> 01:34:32.460
really well.

01:34:32.460 --> 01:34:34.150
So, say, the data from Justin Sonnenburg's
lab.

01:34:34.150 --> 01:34:35.150
And Chris Gardner's lab.

01:34:35.150 --> 01:34:37.699
Tim Ferriss: Enjoy your kilo a day of nattō.

01:34:37.699 --> 01:34:38.770
Tell me how that goes.

01:34:38.770 --> 01:34:40.770
Andrew Huberman: That stuff is so slimy.

01:34:40.770 --> 01:34:42.190
Tim Ferriss: It's so wicked.

01:34:42.190 --> 01:34:46.730
There are two things that — yeah, I've lived
in Japan and was there as an exchange student

01:34:46.730 --> 01:34:51.370
and so on, there are two, well, let's see,
three food items that Japanese people find

01:34:51.370 --> 01:34:55.160
hilarious to watch foreigners try to eat.

01:34:55.160 --> 01:34:57.330
Nattō is definitely number one.

01:34:57.330 --> 01:35:00.869
It's just like spiderweb, cobweb, stink.

01:35:00.869 --> 01:35:02.489
It's so gnarly.

01:35:02.489 --> 01:35:06.730
I can eat it, but it's not my favorite, even
to this day.

01:35:06.730 --> 01:35:12.660
Umeboshi and other types of pickled super,
super salty plums and so on, that's another

01:35:12.660 --> 01:35:13.660
one.

01:35:13.660 --> 01:35:16.800
And then I would say uni also.

01:35:16.800 --> 01:35:19.830
Especially before it was really making the
rounds in the US.

01:35:19.830 --> 01:35:21.560
The first time a foreigner would go over —
Andrew Huberman: — can't do it.

01:35:21.560 --> 01:35:25.090
Tim Ferriss: Just get the seagull poo on top
of the sushi rice.

01:35:25.090 --> 01:35:26.090
It's so hardcore.

01:35:26.090 --> 01:35:27.540
Uni is what I'm talking about.

01:35:27.540 --> 01:35:29.600
Just consistency wise, it's very, very similar.

01:35:29.600 --> 01:35:31.489
Andrew Huberman: It just feels like it's a
tongue.

01:35:31.489 --> 01:35:32.860
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:35:32.860 --> 01:35:34.680
It's not my favorite.

01:35:34.680 --> 01:35:36.600
Andrew Huberman: But people love it.

01:35:36.600 --> 01:35:37.600
Tim Ferriss: Some people do.

01:35:37.600 --> 01:35:39.850
Andrew Huberman: People are told that it's
an aphrodisiac and so they put it —

01:35:39.850 --> 01:35:40.850
Tim Ferriss: That's the best way to sell anything.

01:35:40.850 --> 01:35:42.100
Andrew Huberman: That's right, the best way
of selling.

01:35:42.100 --> 01:35:43.100
That's right.

01:35:43.100 --> 01:35:47.790
Tim Ferriss: I wanted to ask you, because
I have a couple of notes here, tongkat ali,

01:35:47.790 --> 01:35:50.490
Fadogia agrestis, if I'm pronouncing that
correctly.

01:35:50.490 --> 01:35:53.770
But the one I want to ask you about first
is actually, if I'm getting the pronunciation

01:35:53.770 --> 01:35:54.780
correctly, Rhodiola rosea.

01:35:54.780 --> 01:35:56.500
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, Rhodiola rosea.

01:35:56.500 --> 01:35:57.960
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:35:57.960 --> 01:35:59.420
Andrew Huberman: Impressive supplement.

01:35:59.420 --> 01:36:03.820
Tim Ferriss: Please tell me more about this,
because I have heard in my conversations with

01:36:03.820 --> 01:36:12.830
athletes over the years, of people using this
for various endurance purposes, altitude acclimation.

01:36:12.830 --> 01:36:19.119
Andrew Huberman: So Rhodiola rosea is a really
interesting compound because it falls into

01:36:19.119 --> 01:36:22.120
this category of what people call adaptogens.

01:36:22.120 --> 01:36:25.639
But normally when you hear adaptogens, first
of all, that's a very vague term.

01:36:25.639 --> 01:36:28.300
Doesn't actually mean anything specific.

01:36:28.300 --> 01:36:31.930
It means an ability to adapt generally, or
specifically.

01:36:31.930 --> 01:36:34.250
No one's really pinpointed what that means.

01:36:34.250 --> 01:36:36.889
But typically the adaptogens are going to
reduce cortisol.

01:36:36.889 --> 01:36:41.280
So, for instance, Ashwagandha is a very potent
suppressor of cortisol.

01:36:41.280 --> 01:36:45.770
There's some evidence it can indirectly increase
testosterone, but probably through suppression

01:36:45.770 --> 01:36:48.860
of cortisol, since those are in the same synthesis
pathway.

01:36:48.860 --> 01:36:51.449
Ashwagandha is an adaptogen.

01:36:51.449 --> 01:36:56.780
Ashwagandha, because it lowers cortisol, should
probably be taken late day, not early day,

01:36:56.780 --> 01:36:58.820
because you want cortisol high.

01:36:58.820 --> 01:37:04.889
Ashwagandha as a cortisol-suppressing adaptogen,
probably also should not be taken in high

01:37:04.889 --> 01:37:10.520
amounts, not low amounts, but in high amounts,
meaning four to 600 milligrams prior to exercise.

01:37:10.520 --> 01:37:15.410
The whole goal of exercise is to trigger the
adaptation through a spike in cortisol.

01:37:15.410 --> 01:37:17.550
Or one of the goals.

01:37:17.550 --> 01:37:23.310
Rhodiola rosea is a very interesting compound
because it's an adaptogen in that it greatly

01:37:23.310 --> 01:37:28.681
reduces perceived effort and allows for greater
power output and endurance output, as you

01:37:28.681 --> 01:37:34.219
pointed out a moment ago, but it does not
do that by suppressing cortisol.

01:37:34.219 --> 01:37:40.869
So, 200 milligrams of Rhodiola rosea prior
to, say, a resistance-training workout, or

01:37:40.869 --> 01:37:47.900
even on one of your ski tours, you will notice
you have more vigor, you can just go longer,

01:37:47.900 --> 01:37:50.640
and your perceived effort is much lower.

01:37:50.640 --> 01:37:51.640
It's kind of striking.

01:37:51.640 --> 01:37:52.640
Tim Ferriss: What is it doing?

01:37:52.640 --> 01:37:53.700
Andrew Huberman: Now, this is interesting.

01:37:53.700 --> 01:37:58.730
It's kind of striking how after the workout
you don't feel as depleted.

01:37:58.730 --> 01:38:00.590
That perhaps is the reason I started, perhaps
the main reason I started taking it is I found

01:38:00.590 --> 01:38:05.949
I could train harder, but then I suffered
quite a lot from a post-exercise dip in energy.

01:38:05.949 --> 01:38:07.400
Especially if I ate a big meal.

01:38:07.400 --> 01:38:12.810
I no longer experience that if I take Rhodiola
rosea 30 to 60 minutes before workout.

01:38:12.810 --> 01:38:15.780
The effects of it last about four hours.

01:38:15.780 --> 01:38:20.050
So what's happening during that workout, it's
clearly having an effect on the central nervous

01:38:20.050 --> 01:38:26.050
system by reducing the total amount of adrenaline
that's released, or the efficacy of adrenaline

01:38:26.050 --> 01:38:30.449
epinephrine during high intensity effort or
long duration effort.

01:38:30.449 --> 01:38:36.199
So, what this effectively means is, in principle,
one is able to generate the same amount of

01:38:36.199 --> 01:38:41.750
effort without the same amount of energy depleting
neurochemicals.

01:38:41.750 --> 01:38:46.320
I mean, epinephrine, norepinephrine help you
generate energy, but there's always a trough

01:38:46.320 --> 01:38:47.320
afterwards.

01:38:47.320 --> 01:38:48.320
Always.

01:38:48.320 --> 01:38:52.659
And so if you can generate the same amount
of physical output in the absence of X amount

01:38:52.659 --> 01:38:57.250
of adrenaline or norepinephrine, then you're
essentially better off.

01:38:57.250 --> 01:38:59.930
It also seems to catalyze recovery better.

01:38:59.930 --> 01:39:07.250
I would not take it more often than just before
training, however, because there are a few

01:39:07.250 --> 01:39:10.130
studies showing that the effects of it can
kind of taper off if you're taking it all

01:39:10.130 --> 01:39:11.130
day every day.

01:39:11.130 --> 01:39:14.440
Tim Ferriss: Now, would that be true for,
say, Ashwagandha?

01:39:14.440 --> 01:39:16.389
Let's just say someone's taking it before
sleep.

01:39:16.389 --> 01:39:18.110
Would they want to cycle off of that?

01:39:18.110 --> 01:39:21.440
How would you think about cycling if recommended?

01:39:21.440 --> 01:39:22.580
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:39:22.580 --> 01:39:27.260
Low dose of Ashwagandha, 25, 50 milligrams
a day, taking continuously, no problem.

01:39:27.260 --> 01:39:31.210
I actually think AG one has low dose of Ashwagandha
in it.

01:39:31.210 --> 01:39:37.260
But when people are taking Ashwagandha to
offset high stress of mental or physical stuff,

01:39:37.260 --> 01:39:41.880
or both for a period in life, I'd say after
about two weeks, you want at least two weeks

01:39:41.880 --> 01:39:42.880
off.

01:39:42.880 --> 01:39:46.400
You really don't want to suppress cortisol
chronically unless there's some clinical reason

01:39:46.400 --> 01:39:47.780
for that.

01:39:47.780 --> 01:39:55.410
Rhodiola rosea is probably the best addition
to my kind of physical performance stack that

01:39:55.410 --> 01:39:58.320
I've added in a long time, and it's really
striking.

01:39:58.320 --> 01:40:00.450
I mean, I think so much so that people could
try it.

01:40:00.450 --> 01:40:04.659
And it really does seem to work the first
time in every time, for me.

01:40:04.659 --> 01:40:09.780
If it doesn't work for you the first time,
all other things being equal, you got a decent

01:40:09.780 --> 01:40:13.110
night's sleep, you're doing everything in
the same way you normally would and you take

01:40:13.110 --> 01:40:18.250
Rhodiola rosea and you don't really notice
much of an effect, you might try and increase

01:40:18.250 --> 01:40:20.370
the dose slightly and give it another go.

01:40:20.370 --> 01:40:22.370
Tim Ferriss: And what was the dosage range?

01:40:22.370 --> 01:40:24.820
Andrew Huberman: 200 milligrams is what I'll
take.

01:40:24.820 --> 01:40:26.889
And I found that to be really striking.

01:40:26.889 --> 01:40:32.530
Now, I'm not going to take that before a long
Sunday jog or a hike.

01:40:32.530 --> 01:40:35.330
I might, but chances are I'm not going to
do it before the leg workout.

01:40:35.330 --> 01:40:36.989
I'm going to do it before a hit workout.

01:40:36.989 --> 01:40:42.449
Again, of course, I'm mainly doing it to make
sure that I can train really hard and then

01:40:42.449 --> 01:40:44.610
go do other things really hard, too.

01:40:44.610 --> 01:40:50.080
Again, as not a competitive athlete, I loathe
the experience of training really hard and

01:40:50.080 --> 01:40:54.690
then feeling like I gave everything to that
training session and therefore I don't have

01:40:54.690 --> 01:40:57.440
much energy or focus to give to the other
things in life.

01:40:57.440 --> 01:40:59.830
And I think most people are like that.

01:40:59.830 --> 01:41:03.650
Tim Ferriss: Are you still taking the tongkat
ali and the Fadogia agrestis?

01:41:03.650 --> 01:41:07.250
And for those who are not familiar, because
I think we may have made mention of this in

01:41:07.250 --> 01:41:10.520
our last conversation, but just in brief,
what do these two things do?

01:41:10.520 --> 01:41:11.520
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:41:11.520 --> 01:41:14.340
Tongkat ali is an Indonesian ginseng.

01:41:14.340 --> 01:41:18.070
There's a Malaysian version too, but you want
the Indonesian one if you want to pursue these

01:41:18.070 --> 01:41:22.840
effects, which are, it's known to decrease
sex hormone-binding globulin, which frees

01:41:22.840 --> 01:41:25.750
testosterone, which is important in both men
and women.

01:41:25.750 --> 01:41:31.070
It turns out in women there's more testosterone
circulating in a healthy woman, post-puberty

01:41:31.070 --> 01:41:33.520
woman, then there is estrogen.

01:41:33.520 --> 01:41:34.520
Peter Attia taught me this.

01:41:34.520 --> 01:41:39.620
If you normalize for nanograms per deciliter,
women have more testosterone than estrogen,

01:41:39.620 --> 01:41:40.620
healthy women do.

01:41:40.620 --> 01:41:45.770
So, testosterone is associated with libido,
ability, and desire to generate effort, mood,

01:41:45.770 --> 01:41:47.610
et cetera, in men and women.

01:41:47.610 --> 01:41:54.989
Probably the best way to describe testosterone's
effects are it makes effort feel good.

01:41:54.989 --> 01:42:00.850
Tongkat ali frees up more testosterone, mild
libido enhancer for some, more extreme for

01:42:00.850 --> 01:42:04.830
others, increases energy, will increase feelings
of well-being.

01:42:04.830 --> 01:42:12.619
And typically the dosages are 400 milligrams
a day in single dose or divided doses, with

01:42:12.619 --> 01:42:17.730
or without food taken early in the day, before
noon or 2:00 p.m. because it can increase

01:42:17.730 --> 01:42:19.690
energy.You don't want to disrupt your sleep.

01:42:19.690 --> 01:42:21.210
There are a number of good sources of it.

01:42:21.210 --> 01:42:24.400
We can provide links to a couple of those
sources.

01:42:24.400 --> 01:42:25.400
And Fadogia agrestis is a Nigerian shrub.

01:42:25.400 --> 01:42:32.520
It's taken from a Nigerian shrub and it stimulates
the release of luteinizing hormone, which

01:42:32.520 --> 01:42:37.699
is going to come out of the pituitary.

01:42:37.699 --> 01:42:42.350
And in women, will stimulate anything that
comes from downstream of luteinizing hormone

01:42:42.350 --> 01:42:43.350
in the ovary.

01:42:43.350 --> 01:42:46.489
So typically estrogen, maybe even testosterone
to some extent.

01:42:46.489 --> 01:42:50.800
And in men, it will increase testosterone
output from the testes by way of increasing

01:42:50.800 --> 01:42:54.530
luteinizing hormone, maybe subtle increase
in estrogen as well.

01:42:54.530 --> 01:42:55.530
This is important.

01:42:55.530 --> 01:43:00.409
Men hear that something increases estrogen
and they go, "Ooh, I don't want that."

01:43:00.409 --> 01:43:04.159
Well, keep in mind that if you flatline your
estrogen, so if men are taking anastrozole

01:43:04.159 --> 01:43:09.699
or crushing their estrogen, their libido is
going to be zero.

01:43:09.699 --> 01:43:12.010
Their cognitive ability will be diminished.

01:43:12.010 --> 01:43:13.621
Estrogen is important in both men and women.

01:43:13.621 --> 01:43:14.621
Tim Ferriss: It's also cardioprotective.

01:43:14.621 --> 01:43:16.920
Andrew Huberman: Cardioprotective, the endothelial
cells.

01:43:16.920 --> 01:43:20.969
We think of our blood vessels and our arteries
in capillaries as tubes, but they're really

01:43:20.969 --> 01:43:26.250
tubes, imagine Silly Putty kind of rolled
out, Play-Doh made into little flat sheets

01:43:26.250 --> 01:43:29.320
and then rolled up to comprise those tubes.

01:43:29.320 --> 01:43:31.659
So it's many, many endothelial cells that
make up those tubes.

01:43:31.659 --> 01:43:34.280
And the flexibility of those tubes is very
important.

01:43:34.280 --> 01:43:38.889
Obviously, you don't want them rigid, you
need them to expand and contract as needed.

01:43:38.889 --> 01:43:44.120
And estrogen's important for some of that
signaling, leading to that malleability of

01:43:44.120 --> 01:43:45.210
the endothelial cells.

01:43:45.210 --> 01:43:51.199
Fadogia agrestis is typically taken in dosages
of 300 to 600 milligrams per day, with or

01:43:51.199 --> 01:43:52.220
without food.

01:43:52.220 --> 01:43:54.300
Doesn't seem to matter if it's early day,
late day.

01:43:54.300 --> 01:43:58.180
There's some evidence in rats it can be toxic
to the testicular tissue.

01:43:58.180 --> 01:44:01.390
But that's in very, very high concentrations.

01:44:01.390 --> 01:44:05.360
It's interesting, the number of studies on
humans for both tongkat ali and Fadogia agrestis

01:44:05.360 --> 01:44:07.739
have greatly expanded since our last conversation.

01:44:07.739 --> 01:44:11.010
And especially for tongkat ali, there's quite
good support.

01:44:11.010 --> 01:44:13.760
Safety margins are good within the dosage
ranges that we've talked about.

01:44:13.760 --> 01:44:17.170
I've heard of people taking up to a gram a
day of tongkat ali.

01:44:17.170 --> 01:44:18.170
That just makes me cringe.

01:44:18.170 --> 01:44:22.680
I think taking herbal compounds in very high
concentrations is going to be risky no matter

01:44:22.680 --> 01:44:26.630
what, because these things can trigger immune
responses.

01:44:26.630 --> 01:44:32.139
400 milligrams of tongkat ali, 300 to 600
milligrams of Fadogia taken daily should be

01:44:32.139 --> 01:44:33.139
fine.

01:44:33.139 --> 01:44:34.699
I don't cycle them and never have.

01:44:34.699 --> 01:44:36.369
Some people cycle the Fadogia because they're
afraid —

01:44:36.369 --> 01:44:37.369
Tim Ferriss: Why not?

01:44:37.369 --> 01:44:38.590
Andrew Huberman: Why don't I cycle them?

01:44:38.590 --> 01:44:40.139
Because they just keep working.

