02:24:10.350 --> 02:24:14.820
schizophrenic symptoms in those who would
genetically be predisposed.

02:24:14.820 --> 02:24:26.200
So it's not limited to THC, but it is, I think,
under-respected and overused with the assumption

02:24:26.200 --> 02:24:28.021
that it's just cannabis.

02:24:28.021 --> 02:24:30.939
And I think that's a mistake.

02:24:30.939 --> 02:24:32.950
They can be so powerfully psychoactive.

02:24:32.950 --> 02:24:33.950
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

02:24:33.950 --> 02:24:39.020
It's interesting how the proponents of cannabis,
which there are a lot of smart people in cannabis

02:24:39.020 --> 02:24:45.680
medicine, including a lot of MDs, always say
it's not as bad as alcohol.

02:24:45.680 --> 02:24:47.750
Which to me is just a ridiculous argument.

02:24:47.750 --> 02:24:52.100
I mean, to say that something is not as bad
as alcohol implies that you have to choose

02:24:52.100 --> 02:24:53.500
one or the other.

02:24:53.500 --> 02:24:58.310
I think that cannabis has been very beneficial
for a number of adults who are through the

02:24:58.310 --> 02:25:00.180
so-called critical period of brain plasticity.

02:25:00.180 --> 02:25:05.260
So older than 25, they need some way to quote,
unquote, take the edge off in the evening.

02:25:05.260 --> 02:25:07.649
They'll do an edible on the weekend, this
kind of thing.

02:25:07.649 --> 02:25:10.040
That is not what we're talking about in terms
of psychosis.

02:25:10.040 --> 02:25:17.540
We're talking about kids, 12, 13, 14, taking
a bong rip or smoking a joint or vaping a

02:25:17.540 --> 02:25:25.310
super high-potency, THC-containing cannabis
and just being high out of their gourd and

02:25:25.310 --> 02:25:28.390
feeling like it was a really good time, doing
that two or three times.

02:25:28.390 --> 02:25:30.550
And I've seen this over and over again.

02:25:30.550 --> 02:25:34.170
Their parents reach out to me often, in fact,
for whatever reason.

02:25:34.170 --> 02:25:38.960
And then you hear about this person being
17, 18, there's a failure to launch component.

02:25:38.960 --> 02:25:41.620
They're demotivated, they're claiming ADHD.

02:25:41.620 --> 02:25:49.290
They're basically a stoner who relies on cannabis
to relieve anxiety and hasn't done much else

02:25:49.290 --> 02:25:55.561
in the last five years, or certainly has not
managed to keep up with the mean.

02:25:55.561 --> 02:25:57.100
And here I'm sympathetic.

02:25:57.100 --> 02:25:59.410
By the way, you're listening to somebody who
barely finished high school.

02:25:59.410 --> 02:26:03.729
We covered that in the last podcast, so for
other reasons.

02:26:03.729 --> 02:26:06.700
But I think I do worry a lot about these super
high-potency compounds.

02:26:06.700 --> 02:26:08.420
I worry about super high-potency anything.

02:26:08.420 --> 02:26:13.221
I mean, I worry about, in the realm of hormone
augmentation, we're talking Fadogia, tongkat

02:26:13.221 --> 02:26:15.440
ali, not Dianabol.

02:26:15.440 --> 02:26:22.330
In the realm of augmenting mood and focus,
we're comparing very high-potency THC-containing

02:26:22.330 --> 02:26:27.180
cannabis taken in a youth, to 300 milligrams
of alpha GPC.

02:26:27.180 --> 02:26:28.510
This is night and day, right.

02:26:28.510 --> 02:26:31.279
It's chemical augmentation of a completely
different beast.

02:26:31.279 --> 02:26:36.790
So I think that it's clear that no cannabis
is going to be better for most people than

02:26:36.790 --> 02:26:37.890
any cannabis.

02:26:37.890 --> 02:26:40.960
Is occasionally used for an adult going to
be a problem?

02:26:40.960 --> 02:26:42.100
Probably not.

02:26:42.100 --> 02:26:43.100
Probably not.

02:26:43.100 --> 02:26:45.460
Tim Ferriss: I will also say, add to that
a few things.

02:26:45.460 --> 02:26:56.490
Number one, I am deeply interested in the
therapeutic applications of cannabis and all

02:26:56.490 --> 02:26:57.750
of its constituent parts.

02:26:57.750 --> 02:27:04.660
I think it is a very undervalued plant from
that perspective.

02:27:04.660 --> 02:27:11.430
And I think it is severely underestimated
in terms of potency.

02:27:11.430 --> 02:27:20.470
Just the standard available, particularly
in places where you can get the baker's dozen

02:27:20.470 --> 02:27:27.840
of any number of strains, Colorado, et cetera.

02:27:27.840 --> 02:27:36.310
To believe that you are using something of
almost no risk as compared to psychedelics,

02:27:36.310 --> 02:27:39.970
if it contains a lot of THC, is a mistake.

02:27:39.970 --> 02:27:45.210
So I would just say, consider it on par with
some of these very strong psychedelics.

02:27:45.210 --> 02:27:50.680
And just be informed and be cautious about
your use.

02:27:50.680 --> 02:28:00.510
I do think that there are probably some really
significant applications to sleep disorders.

02:28:00.510 --> 02:28:06.580
And I will add, just for people listening,
I have also tried CBN specifically, which

02:28:06.580 --> 02:28:11.109
has been recommended to me for sleep and have
not found it as effective.

02:28:11.109 --> 02:28:16.260
It probably depends on the specific variety
of sleep disorder, but for onset insomnia

02:28:16.260 --> 02:28:22.899
that is a product predominantly not of spike
glucose, well, I guess cortisol and or than

02:28:22.899 --> 02:28:28.290
glucose levels, but rather rumination, THC
seems to be, that seems to be one of the magic

02:28:28.290 --> 02:28:30.311
keys in very low doses.

02:28:30.311 --> 02:28:35.840
But let's segue from that to psychedelics.

02:28:35.840 --> 02:28:46.069
So how has your thinking, any observations,
commentary, beliefs around psychedelics changed

02:28:46.069 --> 02:28:48.500
over the last few years?

02:28:48.500 --> 02:28:54.760
Andrew Huberman: My beliefs and stance, and
also what I'm willing to say, has completely

02:28:54.760 --> 02:28:57.880
changed in the last 24 months.

02:28:57.880 --> 02:29:01.990
This last week, Stanford Magazine, which is
a magazine of the Stanford Alumni Association,

02:29:01.990 --> 02:29:07.069
goes out to many more people than just who
attended Stanford, put out an issue of this,

02:29:07.069 --> 02:29:13.100
it's a very nice bound magazine of the kind
that you would see at the dentist's office.

02:29:13.100 --> 02:29:18.189
On the cover and inside, there's a feature
article about psychedelics.

02:29:18.189 --> 02:29:24.470
I could not believe it at first, but it is
essentially a guide to psychedelics.

02:29:24.470 --> 02:29:27.330
It's not telling you how to do them, but it
explains what is ketamine.

02:29:27.330 --> 02:29:29.609
Now here, they're broadly defining psychedelics.

02:29:29.609 --> 02:29:34.340
So the classic psychedelics like psilocybin,
mescaline, and LSD are included there.

02:29:34.340 --> 02:29:39.270
Chemical structures, how they're used, the
history, clinical trials happening now, known

02:29:39.270 --> 02:29:42.390
benefits, considerations, and risks.

02:29:42.390 --> 02:29:45.710
For all of those drugs, plus ketamine, plus
MDMA.

02:29:45.710 --> 02:29:51.020
I was just shocked, positively shocked because
three years ago, five years ago, certainly

02:29:51.020 --> 02:29:54.440
10 years ago, a conversation like this would've
been the conversation that would've ended

02:29:54.440 --> 02:29:57.680
my career, at least as a university professor.

02:29:57.680 --> 02:30:03.971
My understanding is that thanks to the incredible
work of the group with MAPS and a number of

02:30:03.971 --> 02:30:08.851
specific laboratories, Matthew Johnson's laboratory,
Robin Carhart-Harris' laboratory, Roland Griffiths'

02:30:08.851 --> 02:30:15.069
laboratory, and thanks to the philanthropy
organized by you and others, and also thanks

02:30:15.069 --> 02:30:20.149
to the public education efforts of people
like Michael Pollan, we are moving very quickly

02:30:20.149 --> 02:30:26.859
towards legalization of MDMA as administered
by either psychiatrists and or licensed clinical

02:30:26.859 --> 02:30:28.250
psychologist in the US.

02:30:28.250 --> 02:30:33.670
Psilocybin is probably a longer road, but
I'm told that we'll get there, quote, unquote.

02:30:33.670 --> 02:30:34.950
Why am I framing it this way?

02:30:34.950 --> 02:30:37.500
Well, I definitely have a — I'll just be
very direct.

02:30:37.500 --> 02:30:41.410
In high school, I took LSD recreationally
several times.

02:30:41.410 --> 02:30:42.711
Had not good experiences.

02:30:42.711 --> 02:30:45.100
The experiences were far too long.

02:30:45.100 --> 02:30:46.800
11, 15 hours.

02:30:46.800 --> 02:30:49.640
I might have spent my senior prom in an elevator.

02:30:49.640 --> 02:30:52.020
Tim Ferriss: May or may not, cannot confirm
or deny.

02:30:52.020 --> 02:30:54.160
Andrew Huberman: No, my junior prom.

02:30:54.160 --> 02:30:57.180
My senior prom was a different story that
I definitely, and I'm not recommending people

02:30:57.180 --> 02:30:58.180
do this.

02:30:58.180 --> 02:31:00.640
I actually strongly regret doing that at a
time when my brain was plastic.

02:31:00.640 --> 02:31:01.770
I did not know what I was doing.

02:31:01.770 --> 02:31:03.320
I didn't know the sourcing.

02:31:03.320 --> 02:31:05.220
It was a terrible idea.

02:31:05.220 --> 02:31:06.220
Terrible idea.

02:31:06.220 --> 02:31:07.220
Tim Ferriss: Even riskier now.

02:31:07.220 --> 02:31:08.220
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

02:31:08.220 --> 02:31:09.990
And keep in mind, folks, I was not a star
student, far from it.

02:31:09.990 --> 02:31:12.670
It took a lot of years to get my act together.

02:31:12.670 --> 02:31:15.851
Talked about this before on Tim's podcast,
Rich Roll's podcast, and others.

02:31:15.851 --> 02:31:19.391
So you know, that certainly did not help and
I don't recommend it.

02:31:19.391 --> 02:31:24.510
I tried psilocybin recreationally a few times,
didn't get much out of it.

02:31:24.510 --> 02:31:33.680
As an adult, not shy about the fact that I
did two and now three of the MAPS-appropriate,

02:31:33.680 --> 02:31:37.260
physician-guided sessions for trauma using
MDMA.

02:31:37.260 --> 02:31:38.470
I've found it immensely beneficial.

02:31:38.470 --> 02:31:41.271
I'm happy to talk about how each one of those
sessions was different.

02:31:41.271 --> 02:31:46.069
Again, this is with a physician as part of
a study.

02:31:46.069 --> 02:31:52.810
So what I know now is completely different
than what I knew two years ago, which is not

02:31:52.810 --> 02:31:57.260
just based on the legality, but in discussions
with Nolan Williams, this incredibly impressive

02:31:57.260 --> 02:32:02.240
colleague of mine and friend of yours and
colleague of yours, really, that the safety

02:32:02.240 --> 02:32:05.740
profiles on things like MDMA are actually
quite high.

02:32:05.740 --> 02:32:08.600
I was taught that MDMA was neurotoxic.

02:32:08.600 --> 02:32:09.600
Why was I taught that?

02:32:09.600 --> 02:32:15.521
Well, there's a paper published in Science
magazine looking at toxicity of MDMA, observing

02:32:15.521 --> 02:32:16.521
neurotoxic effects.

02:32:16.521 --> 02:32:17.950
Turns out, what were they looking at?

02:32:17.950 --> 02:32:18.950
Methamphetamine.

02:32:18.950 --> 02:32:19.950
Tim Ferriss: Oops, retraction.

02:32:19.950 --> 02:32:21.300
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, retraction.

02:32:21.300 --> 02:32:22.620
Except that never made the major headlines.

02:32:22.620 --> 02:32:23.620
Tim Ferriss: That never makes it.

02:32:23.620 --> 02:32:25.790
Andrew Huberman: Okay, so then you look at
the data on psilocybin.

02:32:25.790 --> 02:32:27.300
Here, I'm just going to hit the high points.

02:32:27.300 --> 02:32:31.230
Because it is not my work, it's the work of
Matthew Johnson and of Robin Carhart-Harris

02:32:31.230 --> 02:32:32.520
at UCSF.

02:32:32.520 --> 02:32:34.450
Intractable depression.

02:32:34.450 --> 02:32:36.830
People who are suicidally depressed, nothing
else works.

02:32:36.830 --> 02:32:39.490
Talk therapy doesn't work, antidepressants
don't work.

02:32:39.490 --> 02:32:41.320
TMS doesn't work.

02:32:41.320 --> 02:32:47.550
Do two high-dose, so it's 25 milligrams of
psilocybin that has to be translated for grams

02:32:47.550 --> 02:32:48.550
of mushrooms.

02:32:48.550 --> 02:32:50.960
But —
Tim Ferriss: It's roughly like the 25 to 30

02:32:50.960 --> 02:32:54.390
milligrams of psilocybin, synthetic psilocybin
B equivalent.

02:32:54.390 --> 02:32:57.779
Let's just say, a Terence McKenna heroic dose
of five grams.

02:32:57.779 --> 02:32:59.990
I mean, you're getting enough for escape velocity.

02:32:59.990 --> 02:33:01.300
Andrew Huberman: Okay.

02:33:01.300 --> 02:33:05.890
In upwards of 60 percent, maybe 70 percent
of these patients that take that are getting

02:33:05.890 --> 02:33:08.569
substantial and ongoing relief.

02:33:08.569 --> 02:33:10.760
That's an amazing result.

02:33:10.760 --> 02:33:15.890
So much so that the big pharma has moved in,
and is trying to create non-psychedelic psychedelics

02:33:15.890 --> 02:33:20.250
to extract the benefits of these drugs that
don't induce hallucinations.

02:33:20.250 --> 02:33:23.920
Instead of raising interesting questions about
whether or not the experience under psychedelics

02:33:23.920 --> 02:33:28.380
is really the trigger for the antidepressant
effect, whether or not it's the insight or

02:33:28.380 --> 02:33:30.390
not, that's a whole ball of wax.

02:33:30.390 --> 02:33:32.279
And really, I'm not qualified to parse that.

02:33:32.279 --> 02:33:38.500
That's really the domain of Robin and the
psychonauts, and that's an interesting set

02:33:38.500 --> 02:33:40.189
of issues.

02:33:40.189 --> 02:33:45.670
So my stance nowadays is, there is a compound
out there that seems to have very high safety

02:33:45.670 --> 02:33:46.670
profiles.

02:33:46.670 --> 02:33:48.300
Very, very high, certainly for psilocybin.

02:33:48.300 --> 02:33:53.870
That, under the appropriate guidance and supervision
during and after in this so-called integration

02:33:53.870 --> 02:33:59.569
phase, one or two dosages of this stuff, yes,
takes people through a phase of anxiety, then

02:33:59.569 --> 02:34:01.370
a phase of deep introspection.

02:34:01.370 --> 02:34:02.529
This is also, I learned, essential.

02:34:02.529 --> 02:34:06.000
There are two components that I learned that
are essential, that were surprising to me.

02:34:06.000 --> 02:34:08.140
One, you have to be in the eye mask.

02:34:08.140 --> 02:34:12.281
Observing things in your external environment
the whole time seems to bypass some of the

02:34:12.281 --> 02:34:16.260
introspective/antidepressant effects later.

02:34:16.260 --> 02:34:17.260
That's interesting to me.

02:34:17.260 --> 02:34:19.450
It's not just about what you see, what you
hear out there.

02:34:19.450 --> 02:34:22.710
It's really about going inward, this kind
of trust, let go.

02:34:22.710 --> 02:34:23.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:34:23.710 --> 02:34:25.160
It's also important to standardize for trials.

02:34:25.160 --> 02:34:26.160
Right?

02:34:26.160 --> 02:34:28.000
You can't have people looking at all sorts
of different stuff.

02:34:28.000 --> 02:34:30.740
Somebody's watching Finding Nemo, another
one's watching Jaws.

02:34:30.740 --> 02:34:32.229
Andrew Huberman: Right, exactly.

02:34:32.229 --> 02:34:36.920
And then the other thing was, I learned from
Robin recently, is that music seems to be

02:34:36.920 --> 02:34:38.530
a key component.

02:34:38.530 --> 02:34:40.990
Now, they've never teased out music/no music.

02:34:40.990 --> 02:34:45.380
But having music that starts as he described
it in the distance, drums and pacing or something

02:34:45.380 --> 02:34:50.790
approaching music, then instrumentals, which
raise people's emotional state while they're

02:34:50.790 --> 02:34:55.220
in the eye mask, and then some transition
period out, seems to be a critical component

02:34:55.220 --> 02:35:01.140
of all this, and guiding some of the kind
of funneling towards deep emotional introspection.

02:35:01.140 --> 02:35:04.050
I find this incredibly interesting.

02:35:04.050 --> 02:35:08.880
And again, I would've not felt safe talking
about this, keep in mind, a year ago, two

02:35:08.880 --> 02:35:09.880
years ago.

02:35:09.880 --> 02:35:15.090
Keep in mind that in the late '60s, early
'70s, there were professors at Harvard and

02:35:15.090 --> 02:35:20.260
Stanford mainly that were fired, or at least
asked to leave, for having discussions like

02:35:20.260 --> 02:35:21.319
this.

02:35:21.319 --> 02:35:24.319
And now Stanford Magazine itself is printing
this.

02:35:24.319 --> 02:35:26.980
And this is also, I'll use this as an opportunity
to say this.

02:35:26.980 --> 02:35:28.520
Because it's really about the listeners.

02:35:28.520 --> 02:35:32.940
Our Huberman Lab podcast is free on all the
channels.

02:35:32.940 --> 02:35:34.149
But we do have a premium channel.

02:35:34.149 --> 02:35:38.890
I'm not trying to solicit it here, but the
