02:38:24.189 --> 02:38:25.540
hyperfunctional in their life.

02:38:25.540 --> 02:38:27.460
We're not telling you about mystic creatives.

02:38:27.460 --> 02:38:31.560
We're talking about people who, I don't want
to name names, but every single one of them

02:38:31.560 --> 02:38:33.670
already knows this is true.

02:38:33.670 --> 02:38:36.770
Because they've all done this stuff already.

02:38:36.770 --> 02:38:38.830
And I was kind of late to the train.

02:38:38.830 --> 02:38:42.689
Because I had such a terrible experience in
my high school years, and saw so many friends,

02:38:42.689 --> 02:38:46.010
dead, suicide, drug addicts in jail, wrecked
their lives.

02:38:46.010 --> 02:38:52.140
I had so many challenges taking myself from
essentially a loser with nowhere to go to

02:38:52.140 --> 02:38:56.319
a trajectory within academia and taking good
care of my body that I was like, no drugs,

02:38:56.319 --> 02:38:57.569
no drugs.

02:38:57.569 --> 02:38:59.760
I don't even put psychedelics in the category
of drugs.

02:38:59.760 --> 02:39:05.160
And here I'm lumping MDMA in there, provided
it's done with a licensed physician or clinician

02:39:05.160 --> 02:39:06.720
who can guide this stuff.

02:39:06.720 --> 02:39:08.800
It's been immensely beneficial for me.

02:39:08.800 --> 02:39:13.729
Tim Ferriss: What were your different sessions
to the extent that you're willing to share?

02:39:13.729 --> 02:39:20.100
Andrew Huberman: The first one I can just
summarize by saying was extremely somatic.

02:39:20.100 --> 02:39:22.800
Waves of — a lot of shaking.

02:39:22.800 --> 02:39:26.700
At the beginning of the session, I walked
in feeling like I could think and feel things

02:39:26.700 --> 02:39:29.260
from the neck up.

02:39:29.260 --> 02:39:34.100
I could feel things from the waist down, but
that my body wasn't integrated as a system.

02:39:34.100 --> 02:39:41.020
I left that session feeling completely comfortable
in my body as a whole system, and far more

02:39:41.020 --> 02:39:44.100
in tune with my emotions.

02:39:44.100 --> 02:39:47.930
Far more comfortable being, having emotions.

02:39:47.930 --> 02:39:52.210
Far less afraid of what emotions might do
to me, whether or not they were evoked from

02:39:52.210 --> 02:39:53.320
inside or from outside.

02:39:53.320 --> 02:39:58.120
I felt, as a consequence, I felt much braver
in the world.

02:39:58.120 --> 02:40:02.850
And I felt a kind of a healthy adaptive level
of fearlessness, because I felt like nothing

02:40:02.850 --> 02:40:05.770
can hijack my internal emotions.

02:40:05.770 --> 02:40:08.899
And even if they do, I'll be okay.

02:40:08.899 --> 02:40:11.510
And that was a significant thing.

02:40:11.510 --> 02:40:13.189
The second session, very different.

02:40:13.189 --> 02:40:17.010
I expected everything as I did in the first,
never works that way.

02:40:17.010 --> 02:40:26.271
Was one of deep, deep, deep acceptance around
resents that I had for people that I felt

02:40:26.271 --> 02:40:27.800
had neglected me.

02:40:27.800 --> 02:40:30.580
Or just did not do what they needed to do.

02:40:30.580 --> 02:40:34.950
Or that I felt a lot of, and you might be
able to sense a little bit of emotion here.

02:40:34.950 --> 02:40:39.740
It's hard because I can still sense the ways
in which thoughts about that are painful,

02:40:39.740 --> 02:40:42.490
but mostly because it seems so senseless to
me now.

02:40:42.490 --> 02:40:46.240
And yet I have truly zero resent.

02:40:46.240 --> 02:40:49.240
I look for it often, to just check myself
to it.

02:40:49.240 --> 02:40:53.330
No resent, complete forgiveness, which has
given me tremendous relief.

02:40:53.330 --> 02:40:55.601
And then the third session was interesting.

02:40:55.601 --> 02:40:56.880
Third session was a higher dose.

02:40:56.880 --> 02:40:59.980
I had never taken the booster, the MAPS booster.

02:40:59.980 --> 02:41:05.850
Took the booster, and I lay completely still
for about eight hours.

02:41:05.850 --> 02:41:08.010
And it was very introspective.

02:41:08.010 --> 02:41:12.350
And I think I left that session, and I still
maintained from all — everything I've described

02:41:12.350 --> 02:41:16.380
by the way, I feel I've maintained years later
now.

02:41:16.380 --> 02:41:21.810
The third session, which was more recent,
I felt I finally understood and kind of sealed

02:41:21.810 --> 02:41:24.580
up what I can only describe as boundaries.

02:41:24.580 --> 02:41:30.109
That other people can have emotions and experiences
that are truly separate from me.

02:41:30.109 --> 02:41:31.710
I tend to be pretty affiliative.

02:41:31.710 --> 02:41:36.810
I think that came out of an early, especially
with friends and in relationships, it came

02:41:36.810 --> 02:41:41.710
out of an early need to feel some sense of
family where I didn't feel that as much as

02:41:41.710 --> 02:41:45.649
I would've liked from my biological family.

02:41:45.649 --> 02:41:55.060
And there have been times when I've been unable
to really keep in touch with how my life is

02:41:55.060 --> 02:41:58.301
distinct from, and my emotions are distinct
from other peoples'.A little bit too much

02:41:58.301 --> 02:42:00.460
of empathic blurring sometimes.

02:42:00.460 --> 02:42:03.090
And I felt like that was just sealed right
up.

02:42:03.090 --> 02:42:06.990
And mind you, each time I went into these
sessions, I was afraid of all the standard

02:42:06.990 --> 02:42:07.990
things.

02:42:07.990 --> 02:42:08.990
Losing my mind, having a heart attack.

02:42:08.990 --> 02:42:12.420
Now granted, if you have heart issues, tell
your physician you don't want to do MDMA.

02:42:12.420 --> 02:42:14.431
It is an amphetamine in there.

02:42:14.431 --> 02:42:17.670
But I was afraid of all the standard stuff.

02:42:17.670 --> 02:42:24.120
Each time I felt like the window on introspection
and plasticity lasted much longer, weeks longer.

02:42:24.120 --> 02:42:29.120
And each time I just felt like I got better
and better at self-care.

02:42:29.120 --> 02:42:32.650
Which, in my mind, was always a very selfish
thing.

02:42:32.650 --> 02:42:35.580
I always thought self-care is selfish.

02:42:35.580 --> 02:42:38.410
Even working out, I used to hide working out
when I'd go to conferences.

02:42:38.410 --> 02:42:41.010
Because I thought no academic goes to the
gym and lifts weights.

02:42:41.010 --> 02:42:44.630
And I really liked doing that, but I realized
how much stronger I was mentally when I was

02:42:44.630 --> 02:42:46.069
taking care of my physical body.

02:42:46.069 --> 02:42:49.279
But I think that things have changed now.

02:42:49.279 --> 02:42:53.020
So the third session was very meaningful for
me, because I felt like it kind of sealed

02:42:53.020 --> 02:42:54.490
something up.

02:42:54.490 --> 02:42:57.450
Where I go, okay, I'm good.

02:42:57.450 --> 02:43:03.240
Things can happen around me, and I'm not going
to get pulled into it in a way that compromises

02:43:03.240 --> 02:43:05.930
my well-being.

02:43:05.930 --> 02:43:08.080
Of course, I still have a lot in there, and
work to do.

02:43:08.080 --> 02:43:13.160
And Robin Carhart-Harris said to me recently,
he said, "Well, psilocybin is really the honest

02:43:13.160 --> 02:43:14.160
psychedelic.

02:43:14.160 --> 02:43:18.609
Because you don't have the empathogen that's
woven into the MDMA, so you're really going

02:43:18.609 --> 02:43:20.760
to see whatever darkness still exists within
you."

02:43:20.760 --> 02:43:21.800
And I thought, oh, goodness.

02:43:21.800 --> 02:43:25.399
But now I am —
Tim Ferriss: Spoken as a true psilocybin researcher.

02:43:25.399 --> 02:43:26.430
Andrew Huberman: I know.

02:43:26.430 --> 02:43:30.350
So you hear about people, is it calling to
you?

02:43:30.350 --> 02:43:32.990
I'm afraid to do psilocybin for that reason.

02:43:32.990 --> 02:43:38.580
I definitely worked hard to suppress some
of the dark clouds in my head.

02:43:38.580 --> 02:43:41.410
But the fact that I'm still a bit afraid of
them is probably the reason why I should do

02:43:41.410 --> 02:43:42.410
it.

02:43:42.410 --> 02:43:47.391
Tim Ferriss: Well, it also, I think means
you're coming to it from an intellectually

02:43:47.391 --> 02:43:48.510
honest place.

02:43:48.510 --> 02:43:57.640
If someone has absolutely no concerns or misgivings
about extremely powerful psychedelics, I mean,

02:43:57.640 --> 02:44:00.630
you are playing with psychological nuclear
power.

02:44:00.630 --> 02:44:02.880
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, revealing the unconscious.

02:44:02.880 --> 02:44:07.080
I mean, I didn't even know what psychedelic
meant, but I mean Robin taught me, it means

02:44:07.080 --> 02:44:08.790
revealing the unconscious mind.

02:44:08.790 --> 02:44:10.109
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, mind manifesting.

02:44:10.109 --> 02:44:14.859
Andrew Huberman: And I still do extensive
amounts of psychotherapy and psychoanalysis.

02:44:14.859 --> 02:44:16.720
I look at that as just like going to the gym.

02:44:16.720 --> 02:44:21.140
I'm fortunate that my insurance can help pay
for that and I'm able to pay for it, so I

02:44:21.140 --> 02:44:22.140
understand not everybody can.

02:44:22.140 --> 02:44:26.479
But I still feel that if we don't actually
take the time to figure out what's going on

02:44:26.479 --> 02:44:29.550
in our head, how can we really trust that
we're on the best path?

02:44:29.550 --> 02:44:30.550
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:44:30.550 --> 02:44:36.529
Or just representing reality to our benefit,
in a sense.

02:44:36.529 --> 02:44:37.529
Right?

02:44:37.529 --> 02:44:40.720
Because we're perceiving but also constructing
reality.

02:44:40.720 --> 02:44:48.720
And sometimes if you think your glasses are
scratched, or smeared, or foggy or whatever,

02:44:48.720 --> 02:44:50.479
you need to take them off and look at them.

02:44:50.479 --> 02:44:53.790
There are very few ways to do that with your
own psyche.

02:44:53.790 --> 02:44:57.140
There are very few tools that allow you to
do that.

02:44:57.140 --> 02:45:01.470
And I would recommend for folks if they are
interested in, and thank you for being so

02:45:01.470 --> 02:45:09.819
forthcoming with describing your experiences,
getting a good overview of how MDMA specifically

02:45:09.819 --> 02:45:13.600
can help with say, complex PTSD.

02:45:13.600 --> 02:45:19.020
And why it has become such a focal point for
many, many researchers and practitioners.

02:45:19.020 --> 02:45:26.220
I do recommend the Netflix, How to Change
Your Mind miniseries that Pollan was involved

02:45:26.220 --> 02:45:29.390
with based on the book of the same name.

02:45:29.390 --> 02:45:33.660
And specifically, the MDMA episode I thought
was spectacularly well-done.

02:45:33.660 --> 02:45:35.149
Andrew Huberman: I need to see that, still.

02:45:35.149 --> 02:45:40.140
Tim Ferriss: It's very well done, and includes
a lot of case studies in interviews with research

02:45:40.140 --> 02:45:41.149
subjects.

02:45:41.149 --> 02:45:46.780
And there's also a documentary that I helped
bring to, well, outside of Israel called Trip

02:45:46.780 --> 02:45:47.780
of Compassion.

02:45:47.780 --> 02:45:53.530
This was probably four or five years ago,
which contains session footage as well.

02:45:53.530 --> 02:45:57.101
Production value is going to be a bit lower
than Netflix, so you might want to try that

02:45:57.101 --> 02:45:58.101
first.

02:45:58.101 --> 02:46:01.170
But there are a number of forthcoming books
coming that'll be focused on these topics.

02:46:01.170 --> 02:46:02.460
A lot of good stuff happening.

02:46:02.460 --> 02:46:03.660
Andrew Huberman: It's exciting.

02:46:03.660 --> 02:46:07.600
Tim Ferriss: In terms of investigation, there
are risks.

02:46:07.600 --> 02:46:11.030
There are very non-trivial risks.

02:46:11.030 --> 02:46:20.050
And that is part of the reason why the fundamental
research is important, which is why people

02:46:20.050 --> 02:46:25.170
like, I have to mention Roland because he
was my gateway into so much of this.

02:46:25.170 --> 02:46:32.520
Roland Griffiths, and Robin, and Nolan and
others are doing very, very important work.

02:46:32.520 --> 02:46:39.979
Because there's the commercialization, there's
the development of derivatives and perhaps

02:46:39.979 --> 02:46:41.490
non-psychedelic options.

02:46:41.490 --> 02:46:49.640
And I think that they hold promise in certain
conditions like cluster headaches, for instance.

02:46:49.640 --> 02:46:57.090
However, I do firmly fall into the camp that
believes there are these mechanistic, receptor-level

02:46:57.090 --> 02:46:58.090
effects.

02:46:58.090 --> 02:47:05.600
And many other physiological, measurable effects
that exert or impart some of the benefits

02:47:05.600 --> 02:47:09.180
that we document in trials.

02:47:09.180 --> 02:47:14.351
But I am firmly in the camp that the content
matters, deeply.

02:47:14.351 --> 02:47:21.490
Andrew Huberman: Robin and I and Nolan and
I also discussed things like iboga, ibogaine,

02:47:21.490 --> 02:47:27.529
22-hour long psychedelic journey, no hallucinations
with the eyes open, close the eyes, see — drop

02:47:27.529 --> 02:47:31.010
into very vivid imagery of previous experiences.

02:47:31.010 --> 02:47:33.740
That doesn't interest me so much, it seems
a little bit much.

02:47:33.740 --> 02:47:36.439
It's going to be hard also to do clinical
studies on that.

02:47:36.439 --> 02:47:40.569
As Robin pointed out, one of the reasons why
most of the trials are being done with psilocybin

02:47:40.569 --> 02:47:42.980
or MDMA is because the sessions are four to
six hours.

02:47:42.980 --> 02:47:48.700
With some aftercare LSD, double or triple
that.

02:47:48.700 --> 02:47:53.760
And of course, we should point out that street
MDMA could very well be, and is often laced

02:47:53.760 --> 02:47:54.760
with fentanyl.

02:47:54.760 --> 02:47:56.880
So the sourcing here must be through MAPS.

02:47:56.880 --> 02:48:01.370
I think they're the only ones that actually
have the clean sourcing of MDMA.

02:48:01.370 --> 02:48:03.160
I know people out there will be like, "I know
a clean source."

02:48:03.160 --> 02:48:06.290
But nowadays I think people need to be exceedingly
cautious about fentanyl because it's deadly.

02:48:06.290 --> 02:48:07.510
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, there's a great — I just
want to butt in for a second.

02:48:07.510 --> 02:48:12.561
There is an organization called DanceSafe,
which I encourage people to check out, which

02:48:12.561 --> 02:48:14.460
provides kits.

02:48:14.460 --> 02:48:20.270
Because I know that you know can tell kids,
"Abstain, no sex, no sex, just no sex until

02:48:20.270 --> 02:48:21.270
you're married."

02:48:21.270 --> 02:48:22.270
They're going to have sex anyway.

02:48:22.270 --> 02:48:28.120
So I recognize people who are listening are
probably going to use drugs from unclear sourcing,

02:48:28.120 --> 02:48:30.710
or it will be a game of telephone.

02:48:30.710 --> 02:48:34.610
It'll be from five different, 10 different,
12 different, a hundred different hands to

02:48:34.610 --> 02:48:35.610
theirs.

02:48:35.610 --> 02:48:37.200
DanceSafe has kits.

02:48:37.200 --> 02:48:41.649
I'm not recommending that you use drugs in
that fashion, but people are going to do it

02:48:41.649 --> 02:48:42.649
anyway.

02:48:42.649 --> 02:48:47.010
There are kits you can purchase which will
help you to detect some of these contaminants.

02:48:47.010 --> 02:48:50.240
So at the very least do that type of due diligence.

02:48:50.240 --> 02:48:51.830
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, yeah.

02:48:51.830 --> 02:48:56.689
So you asked how do I feel about these compounds
done in the clinical setting and how has my

02:48:56.689 --> 02:48:59.550
stance changed?

02:48:59.550 --> 02:49:00.550
Complete 180.

02:49:00.550 --> 02:49:01.550
Complete 180.

02:49:01.550 --> 02:49:02.550
I'm super curious.

02:49:02.550 --> 02:49:03.550
I'm super excited.

02:49:03.550 --> 02:49:07.590
And least of all for me, I'm excited about
what, my experience, it was very positive

02:49:07.590 --> 02:49:08.590
all around.

02:49:08.590 --> 02:49:13.109
But I'm most excited for the millions and
millions, maybe even billions of people out

02:49:13.109 --> 02:49:14.109
there.

02:49:14.109 --> 02:49:18.670
Billions of people certainly have trauma,
but the millions of people who are impaired,

02:49:18.670 --> 02:49:22.910
in terms of daily mood and basic functioning
because of depression, anxiety.

02:49:22.910 --> 02:49:29.540
I mean, there are psilocybin trials for fibromyalgia,
for anorexia nervosa, the most deadly psychiatric

02:49:29.540 --> 02:49:30.590
illness is anorexia.

02:49:30.590 --> 02:49:35.770
Of course, people with bipolar or schizophrenia,
these are not good candidates because of the

02:49:35.770 --> 02:49:38.890
propensity for exacerbating psychosis or for
manic episodes.

02:49:38.890 --> 02:49:43.189
But I think we are heading into really interesting
times.

02:49:43.189 --> 02:49:46.430
And a year or two ago, I would've thought,
ah, this isn't going to make it through the

02:49:46.430 --> 02:49:47.430
chute.

02:49:47.430 --> 02:49:49.510
Something's going to happen and the whole
thing's going to fall apart.

02:49:49.510 --> 02:49:53.250
I think it was the event that we were both
at, the Veterans Solutions event on Coronado

02:49:53.250 --> 02:49:54.250
Island.

02:49:54.250 --> 02:49:58.140
It's an incredible group that you're associated
with, and we have common friends from the

02:49:58.140 --> 02:50:03.729
special operations community who are doing
these iboga DMT combined sessions out of country,

02:50:03.729 --> 02:50:08.200
and then working with Nolan Williams to look
at how the brain changes.

02:50:08.200 --> 02:50:13.460
And we saw Governor Rick Perry there, a pretty
right-wing guy.

02:50:13.460 --> 02:50:16.230
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he interviewed Rick and
Rick.

02:50:16.230 --> 02:50:18.340
About as far apart, politically, as you can
be.

02:50:18.340 --> 02:50:21.470
Andrew Huberman: And then Rick Doblin, who
stood up there and said in front of a room

02:50:21.470 --> 02:50:25.510
full of special operators that he'd been a
conscientious objector during the Vietnam

02:50:25.510 --> 02:50:26.510
War.

02:50:26.510 --> 02:50:29.150
And I thought, wow, if he doesn't get killed
in this room, he's going to survive forever.

02:50:29.150 --> 02:50:32.740
In other words, polar extremes politically.

02:50:32.740 --> 02:50:37.819
Standing there talking about psychedelics
and their value for treatment of intractable

02:50:37.819 --> 02:50:43.760
depression, trauma, offsetting the certain
amount of suicide risk in many individuals.

02:50:43.760 --> 02:50:47.510
Talking about, I think I heard Rick Perry
say the words "heart medicine."

02:50:47.510 --> 02:50:49.220
I almost fell out of my chair.

02:50:49.220 --> 02:50:50.761
I mean, this is super exciting.

02:50:50.761 --> 02:50:55.950
And it's super exciting because, the only
way to describe it is the way that Paul Conti

02:50:55.950 --> 02:50:57.300
sometimes describes things.

02:50:57.300 --> 02:51:02.479
There's certain things for which there's a
lot of work, a lot of potential hazards, but

02:51:02.479 --> 02:51:05.890
that done properly — and he wasn't referring
to psychedelics when he said this, but this

02:51:05.890 --> 02:51:09.440
particular, but there's so much goodness that
could come out of this.

02:51:09.440 --> 02:51:13.670
The amount of goodness that could come out
of everyone having access to great medical

02:51:13.670 --> 02:51:17.120
care, therapy, and potentially psychedelic
therapies.

02:51:17.120 --> 02:51:20.220
I mean, just imagine the magnitude of the
world change.

02:51:20.220 --> 02:51:23.100
That's the kind of stuff that gets chills
running up my spine.

02:51:23.100 --> 02:51:27.330
And you just think, well, gosh, but the scale
of that problem is just too big, legally,

02:51:27.330 --> 02:51:28.330
financially.

02:51:28.330 --> 02:51:32.399
But enough has happened now that at least
some of that, in terms of psychedelics, could

02:51:32.399 --> 02:51:33.680
very well happen.

02:51:33.680 --> 02:51:37.050
And again, to clarify, Paul Conti wasn't talking
about psychedelics.

02:51:37.050 --> 02:51:42.210
He just has this, occasionally he'll say something
— we're doing a series with him on mental

02:51:42.210 --> 02:51:43.210
health.

02:51:43.210 --> 02:51:47.210
And occasionally he'll say something like,
"Certain things are just goodness."

02:51:47.210 --> 02:51:50.791
And I love the sound of that because it sounds,
there's something so wholesome about it, but

02:51:50.791 --> 02:51:51.791
so real.

02:51:51.791 --> 02:51:57.370
I mean, waking up and feeling good enough
to pursue the basic events of the day, with

02:51:57.370 --> 02:52:02.859
some hardship of course from time to time,
good enough to set a goal, and fail, and then

02:52:02.859 --> 02:52:03.859
try again.

02:52:03.859 --> 02:52:10.430
Good enough to have a rough run through a
relationship, or a hard family life, or lose

02:52:10.430 --> 02:52:12.521
all your money and go bankrupt and come back.

02:52:12.521 --> 02:52:14.780
That's the stuff of real life.

02:52:14.780 --> 02:52:20.130
And I think that there's so many so-called
deaths of despair, and then there are 10 times

02:52:20.130 --> 02:52:23.239
more sort of lives of despair out there.

02:52:23.239 --> 02:52:28.680
And I think there's seriously the opportunity
for real healing, for lack of a better word.

02:52:28.680 --> 02:52:31.029
Tim Ferriss: High leverage, very high leverage.

02:52:31.029 --> 02:52:33.029
I'm excited to see where it goes.

02:52:33.029 --> 02:52:40.729
And I would tell people also, don't be shy
about following the money.

02:52:40.729 --> 02:52:50.590
In the sense that, there will be every possible
attempt to contort and change psychedelic

02:52:50.590 --> 02:52:53.930
therapies to fit into existing healthcare.

02:52:53.930 --> 02:52:57.300
And I do think there's upside to that.

02:52:57.300 --> 02:53:06.560
Not everyone is going to be able to afford
whatever the costs may be to receive bespoke

02:53:06.560 --> 02:53:10.130
medical care with a session that lasts 18
hours.

02:53:10.130 --> 02:53:14.370
Or whatever it is, 12 hours, let's just say
in the case of the sort of upper range for

02:53:14.370 --> 02:53:17.060
some people with LSD.

02:53:17.060 --> 02:53:23.979
However, if you see a company that's saying
5-MeO-DMT is the ticket because it's the businessman's

02:53:23.979 --> 02:53:30.300
psychedelic therapy because it lasts 15 minutes,
take a sniff test or two first.

02:53:30.300 --> 02:53:33.990
And also just because it's 15 minutes Earth-time
doesn't mean your experience 15 minutes.

02:53:33.990 --> 02:53:36.960
So just be cautious with that.

02:53:36.960 --> 02:53:45.100
The removal of psychedelic effects also makes
it much more adaptable, and plug-and-play

02:53:45.100 --> 02:53:51.489
with current healthcare practitioners and
things can be turned into maintenance doses.

02:53:51.489 --> 02:53:52.760
Andrew Huberman: Or for kids.

02:53:52.760 --> 02:53:54.290
Tim Ferriss: Or for kids, right.

02:53:54.290 --> 02:53:58.520
So you say, if you take generic ketamine,
and I'm not going to name too many names,

02:53:58.520 --> 02:54:06.060
but some of the sort of slight molecular changes
that have been made to create, say, maintenance

02:54:06.060 --> 02:54:09.760
doses with nasal sprays.

02:54:09.760 --> 02:54:14.920
I do think that, at least for me, and I may
be old-fashioned, but I do think that these

02:54:14.920 --> 02:54:20.821
compounds, in relatively few sessions, have
the potential to induce plasticity, provide

02:54:20.821 --> 02:54:28.760
experiences that have — I'm not going to
say, well, in some cases curative capability

02:54:28.760 --> 02:54:32.240
or potential, but extremely high durability.

02:54:32.240 --> 02:54:34.350
We're talking about the order of years.

02:54:34.350 --> 02:54:39.390
So I think people should be skeptical of companies
that aim to create maintenance drugs from

02:54:39.390 --> 02:54:40.390
these.

02:54:40.390 --> 02:54:46.050
Although that could be applicable to certain
populations that, say, may fit into exclusionary

02:54:46.050 --> 02:54:51.260
criteria because they can't take higher doses
for risk of say, psychosis.

02:54:51.260 --> 02:54:55.930
But they might be able to take very, very
low doses along the lines of, say, a microdose.

02:54:55.930 --> 02:55:01.850
Which wouldn't impart the perhaps content
experiences, but could have some effects on

02:55:01.850 --> 02:55:07.770
say, serotonin type 2A receptors, but intracellularly,
which I was just reading about.

02:55:07.770 --> 02:55:11.729
I would recommend if people want to keep up
to speed with these things, The Microdose

02:55:11.729 --> 02:55:14.410
from UC Berkeley is fantastic.

02:55:14.410 --> 02:55:17.899
That's sort of a news bulletin summary on
a weekly basis.

02:55:17.899 --> 02:55:22.439
If you want a good overview, I think How to
Change Your Mind is a great place to start.

02:55:22.439 --> 02:55:25.480
If you want to know how some psychotherapists
have worked with these things, I think an

02:55:25.480 --> 02:55:29.859
oldie but a goodie, The Healing Journey by
Claudio Naranjo, I think is excellent.

02:55:29.859 --> 02:55:33.630
Even if you just buy it for the introduction,
I think MAPS sells that.

02:55:33.630 --> 02:55:35.350
So a lot of good resources out there.

